    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/194267": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Instruction",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Department of Chemistry Assistant Professor of Instruction - General Chemistry Apply now Description The Department of Chemistry at the University of Texas at Austin is recruiting qualified candidates for a full-time, non-tenure track faculty position with primary teaching responsibilities in general chemistry. Primary duties for this position include teaching multiple sections of large-enrollment undergraduate general chemistry courses. The successful candidate will work closely with the General Chemistry instructional team to coordinate teaching activities, support curricular innovation and continuous improvement, and promote student success across the program. Additional responsibilities include participating in course development activities, conducting regular office hours and review sessions, and training, supervising, mentoring, and instructing teaching assistants. All ranks carry a nine-month appointment, renewed annually based on demonstrated teaching excellence and need of the department. Summer teaching appointments are often available but teaching assignments may vary. Qualifications PhD in chemistry or related field is required. Experience teaching at the undergraduate level is preferred. Application Instructions Upload cover letter, current CV, and teaching statement to Interfolio.  Enter names and contact information for at least 3 references who can write letters attesting to your ability as an instructor. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Department of Chemistry Posted Oct 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/194100": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Applied Linguistics",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor in Applied Linguistics – German – Department of European and Eurasian Studies Apply now Description The Department of European and Eurasian Studies at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor to begin Fall of academic year 2027-2028. We seek a specialist in German Applied Linguistics, with complementary research and teaching in Second Language Acquisition, Psycholinguistics, Digital Humanities, multilingualism, or language contact in the European context, with the charge of overseeing Germanic Studies graduate student professionalization and directing the lower-division German language program. Duties will include undergraduate teaching (including teaching the Core Curriculum), graduate teaching, research, and service to the department, college, university, and the field. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the Ph.D. by time of appointment, or expect to obtain the Ph.D. within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor.  Near-native fluency in both German and English is required. Preferred qualifications include: The ideal candidate will have an active, data-driven research program in areas such as language acquisition (including lab-based, classroom-based, and digital approaches to research), textual analysis, reading/writing studies, learning theory, outcomes assessment, corpus linguistics, or critical discourse analysis, including research design in their fields; a track record of TA training and supervision (potentially including those in languages other than German, but with a principal focus on Germanic languages); a demonstrated long-term commitment to making and maintaining ties with language teaching on the level of national professional organizations (e.g., AATG, AAUSC, ACTFL, and AAAL) as well as locally (local AATG chapters; campus service in placement, assessment, language events on campus); the ablity to contribute to and offer courses for the Texas Language Center (TLC) Portfolio Certificate in Language Teaching / Language Program Coordination. Application Instructions To apply, upload a letter of interest, CV, names and contact information for three recommenders, teaching portfolio (including at least three sets of teaching evaluations, at least one of which should be for a lower-division German course), and a writing sample.  Additional materials may be requested after the initial review of applications. Applications must be received no later than November 23, 2026. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For further information, please contact: Marc Pierce, Search Committee Chair mpierc@austin.utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of European and Eurasian Studies Posted Oct 13, 2026 Apply by Nov 23, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/194057": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Architecture AY27-29: Emerging Scholar Fellowship in Design Apply now Description The University of Texas at Austin School of Architecture invites applications and nominations for its 2027-29 Emerging Scholar in Design. The prestigious two-year fellowship offers promising individuals at the beginning of their academic careers in architecture and its allied fields the opportunity to develop a distinctive body of work, and provides a unique teaching experience in the context of a supportive academic environment. The school is seeking a candidate that is highly motivated and positioned to integrate their scholarly trajectory with their pedagogical agenda. The fellowship is part of the School's growing Emerging Scholars Program, which aims to build a community of fellows over time. Scholars join a cohort supported by faculty mentorship, shared programming, and opportunities for exchange with one another and with the broader School of Architecture community. The scholar will have a reduced teaching load of three courses per academic year, typically two design studios and one elective in their area of expertise. Depending on the scholar's expertise and the school's curricular needs, core courses may be assigned in place of design studios. Teaching responsibilities may involve both graduate and undergraduate programs. In addition to teaching, the successful candidate is expected to contribute to the school's intellectual life by engaging in critical theoretical or creative work, which they will develop during their time as the emerging scholar and present to the university community in the last semester of their appointment through a public format, such as a lecture or exhibition. Background The School of Architecture offers professionally accredited and post-professional degree programs in Architecture, Interior Design, Landscape Architecture, and Community and Regional Planning, as well as advanced research degrees in Architectural History, Historic Preservation, Sustainable Design, and Urban Design. With approximately 700 students across its programs, the School benefits from a strong studio culture, research-oriented initiatives, and an engaged community of faculty and students. Faculty at the School benefit from access to a suite of labs that support advanced teaching and research. The Materials Lab houses one of the largest institutional collections of material samples, available for teaching, research, and public exhibitions. The Technology Lab provides equipment for digital simulation and visualization, including VR/AR tools. The Build Lab supports full-scale prototyping construction, and fabrication. These resources enable faculty to integrate design, research, and experimentation into their teaching and practice. As one of the largest and most highly regarded public universities in the United States, The University of Texas at Austin offers faculty access to extensive infrastructure and research networks. The university is home to more than 200 research centers and units and is committed to fostering interdisciplinary work across its colleges and schools. Faculty at UT benefit from both internal and external research opportunities, as well as robust support for teaching and professional development. Austin offers a unique context for the study of the built environment. As the 12th most populous city in the United States with a population of over one million - the anchor of a rapidly growing megaregion - Austin is experiencing rapid urban transformation, providing a living laboratory for faculty research, creative practice, and teaching. The city's cultural vibrancy, entrepreneurial environment, and rich natural surroundings, combined with its challenges of growth, affordability, and environmental vulnerability, provide fertile ground for engaged scholarship and professional education. Qualifications Application Instructions Application review will begin on January 15, 2027 and will continue until the position is filled. To be considered for the Emerging Scholar in Design, please submit the following materials through Interfolio: A statement of interest describing your research and teaching objectives for the fellowship. A current CV. A PDF portfolio of creative work or scholarship not to exceed 20 pages. Contact information for three references. If you have any questions, please contact the search committee chair, Associate Professor Igor Siddiqui at igor.siddiqui@utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Architecture Posted Oct 13, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193994": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Dell Medical School Ophthalmologist Apply now Description About Dell Medical School As part of The University of Texas at Austin, one of the nation’s leading research universities, the Dell Medical School pursues innovation in the redesign of healthcare delivery, excellence in healthcare research, and programs in interdisciplinary and inter-professional education. It is the fifth medical school in the UT System and the first medical school established in nearly five decades by a member of the Association of American Universities, an organization of leading public and private research universities. The University of Texas Medical Center, anchored by Dell Medical School, is defining the future of health. Announced in August 2023 with planning and implementation underway, The University of Texas Medical Center will cement Austin as a premier destination for health care alongside other major Texas cities. With two new, state-of-the-art hospitals, a UT specialty hospital and a cancer center being built by MD Anderson, plus the academic and research expertise of UT, The University of Texas Medical Center will provide the integrated, world-class care that is a hallmark of academic medicine. The Dell Medical School is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to excellence within the learning health system. About the Department of Ophthalmology The Department of Ophthalmology at Dell Medical School supports high-quality ophthalmic patient care, education, and academic activities. Faculty work collaboratively across subspecialties and with referring providers while contributing to the clinical and educational missions of an academic medical center. About the Ophthalmologist Position The University of Texas at Austin Dell Medical School is seeking a fellowship trained ophthalmologist to join the Department of Ophthalmology and the Mitchel and Shannon Wong Eye Institute (MSWEI). Qualified candidates will be considered at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor, Professional Track, or Professor Title Series (nontenured). The Department of Ophthalmology and MSWEI seek a dynamic, academically oriented, mission-driven faculty member with a commitment to outstanding patient care, education, and scholarly activity. This is a 100% FTE position. Essential Functions: Provide comprehensive medical and surgical ophthalmic care to patients across a broad range of ophthalmic conditions, including patients in their subspecialty area. Deliver evidence-based, patient-centered care and participate in clinical innovation and care redesign initiatives. Participate in the education and training of medical students, residents, and other learners in the diagnosis and clinical and surgical management of ophthalmic disease. Engage in scholarly activity, clinical research, quality improvement initiatives, or other academic pursuits aligned with the mission of Dell Medical School. Contribute to the strategic growth and development of the Department of Ophthalmology and MSWEI. Develop collaborative relationships with referring physicians, hospitals, and community partners throughout Austin and Central Texas. Participate in departmental service, committees, and other academic activities as appropriate. Other Possible Functions: Opportunities for leadership roles may be available based on qualifications, experience, and departmental needs. Candidates with interest in fellowship development, research leadership, or programmatic growth are encouraged to apply. Qualifications Required: Medical degree from an accredited medical school Current Board certiﬁcation/eligibility in Ophthalmology Fellowship training in either Cornea/External Disease, Glaucoma, Uveitis or Medical Retina Eligible for medical licensure in Texas Eligible for faculty appointment with The University of Texas at Austin Dell Medical School Demonstrated commitment to patient care, education, and academic medicine. Preferred: Record of leadership, scholarly achievement, research, or educational contributions. Experience teaching residents, fellows, and medical students. Academic or clinical program development experience. #healthstartshere2 Application Instructions Interested and qualified candidates should complete the required application and submit a current CV. #healthstartshere2 Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Dell Medical School Department Department of Ophthalmology Posted Oct 9, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193991": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Dell Medical School Clinical Ophthalmologist Apply now Description About Dell Medical School As part of The University of Texas at Austin, one of the nation’s leading research universities, the Dell Medical School pursues innovation in the redesign of healthcare delivery, excellence in healthcare research, and programs in interdisciplinary and inter-professional education. It is the fifth medical school in the UT System and the first medical school established in nearly five decades by a member of the Association of American Universities, an organization of leading public and private research universities. The University of Texas Medical Center, anchored by Dell Medical School, is defining the future of health. Announced in August 2023 with planning and implementation underway, The University of Texas Medical Center will cement Austin as a premier destination for health care alongside other major Texas cities. With two new, state-of-the-art hospitals, a UT specialty hospital and a cancer center being built by MD Anderson, plus the academic and research expertise of UT, The University of Texas Medical Center will provide the integrated, world-class care that is a hallmark of academic medicine. The Dell Medical School is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to excellence within the learning health system. About the Department of Ophthalmology The Department of Ophthalmology at Dell Medical School supports high-quality ophthalmic patient care, education, and academic activities. Faculty work collaboratively across subspecialties and with referring providers while contributing to the clinical and educational missions of an academic medical center. About the Clinical Ophthalmologist Position The Clinical Assistant Professor in the Department of Ophthalmology will provide attending oversight for patient care delivered by residents in the resident clinic. The faculty member will supervise residents in the evaluation, diagnosis, and management of ophthalmic conditions; support clinical decision-making and care planning; and ensure appropriate attending review and documentation. This role is well suited to a clinician with a strong commitment to resident education, safe and high-quality patient care, and collaborative practice within an academic medical center. Key Responsibilities Provide attending oversight and supervision for patient care delivered by residents in the resident clinic. Review resident evaluations, diagnoses, treatment plans, and clinical documentation to ensure safe, high-quality patient care. Serve as a clinical resource and mentor to residents, providing real-time guidance, teaching, and feedback during patient encounters. Support resident development through observation, coaching, and assessment of clinical performance. Collaborate with faculty, clinical staff, and referring providers to support coordinated patient care and resident training. Maintain timely and accurate documentation and complete required attending attestations and reviews. Participate in departmental meetings, quality improvement initiatives, and other activities related to resident education and clinic operations as appropriate. Live in Austin, a Vibrant University City Known for its excellent schools, lively music scene, superb cuisine, and international festivals such as South by Southwest, Austin typically ranks among the country’s best places to live. Nearby lakes and rivers, as well as the adjacent Texas Hill Country, provide ample opportunities for outdoor activities. Why Join Our Team We offer an attractive compensation and benefits package that is well rounded and competitive, making it desirable for individuals with excellent clinical skills, a passion for education, and potential for scholarly contributions to join a dynamic department in an innovative and growing medical school. Qualifications Required M.D. or D.O. degree from an accredited medical school. Successful completion of an accredited residency program in Ophthalmology. Board certified or board eligible in Ophthalmology through the American Board of Ophthalmology or equivalent. Eligible for, or currently holding, an unrestricted medical license in the state of practice. Eligible for medical staff privileges at affiliated clinical sites. Strong interpersonal, communication, and teaching skills. Preferred Fellowship training in a relevant ophthalmic subspecialty, such as cornea, retina, glaucoma, pediatric ophthalmology, oculoplastics, or neuro-ophthalmology. Significant clinical experience in ophthalmology and a demonstrated ability to provide clinical oversight, mentorship, and guidance to resident physicians Demonstrated interest or experience in clinical research or medical education. Application Instructions Interested and qualified candidates should complete the required application and submit a current CV. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Dell Medical School Department Department of Ophthalmology Posted Oct 9, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193909": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor- Advanced Computational Geotechnics- Fall 2027",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Assistant Professor- Advanced Computational Geotechnics- Fall 2027 Apply now Description The Maseeh Department of Civil, Architectural, and Environmental Engineering (CAEE) at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position in geotechnical engineering with expertise in advanced computational geotechnics. The candidate’s research should address innovative computational methods for analyzing, modeling, and predicting the behavior of geotechnical systems across multiple spatial and temporal scales. Specific research areas of interest may include: advanced computational methods, constitutive and multi-physics modeling, digital twins and predictive simulation, data-enabled computational geomechanics, subsurface data interpretation and modeling, and computational modeling of resilient infrastructure systems. We are committed to supporting the success of all members of our community: students, staff, and faculty.  A successful candidate can expect to benefit from and contribute to those efforts. Qualifications Candidates must have a PhD in engineering or a related area. The successful candidate will be expected to create undergraduate and graduate learning environments that address the needs of students from a variety of backgrounds with differing learning styles and abilities. Further, the successful applicant will be expected to develop an externally sponsored research program, mentor graduate students, collaborate with other faculty, and serve the university and profession. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit the following materials in Interfolio : (1) a cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) research statement, (4) teaching and mentoring statement, and (5) a list of three references. Please see CAEE Faculy Openings for additional information and guidance. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Please address questions to Prof. Charles J. Werth, CAEE Department Chair ( werth@utexas.edu ). Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026 , and will continue until an appropriate candidate is identified. Further information on the Maseeh Department of Civil, Architectural, and Environmental Engineering may be found at the department webpage . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Civil, Architectural & Environmental Engineering Posted Oct 8, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193908": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor- Engineering for the Extreme",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Assistant Professor- Engineering for the Extreme - Fall 2027 Apply now Description The Maseeh Department of Civil, Architectural, and Environmental Engineering (CAEE) at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position in Engineering for the Extreme. We are interested in candidates whose research addresses the multitude of challenges in engineering for the extreme, at both the system and material levels, including: (a) design for extreme terrestrial (subsea, underground, polar, desert) or extraterrestrial (lunar, Martian, orbital) settings; (b) design strategies to enhance resilience to extreme natural (e.g., atmospheric) or anthropogenic events (e.g., wildfires); and/or (c) design for extreme or uncommon stressors (e.g., radiation, intense thermal cycling, extreme pressures, micrometeorite impacts). Candidates leveraging AI and physics-informed computational modeling in novel ways for engineering for the extreme are strongly encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will have access to various experimental and high-performance computing facilities. We are committed to supporting the success of all members of our community: students, staff, and faculty.  A successful candidate can expect to benefit from and contribute to those efforts. Qualifications Candidates must have a PhD in engineering or a related area. The successful candidate will be expected to create undergraduate and graduate learning environments that address the needs of students from a variety of backgrounds with differing learning styles and abilities. Further, the successful applicant will be expected to develop an externally sponsored research program, mentor graduate students, collaborate with other faculty, and serve the university and profession. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit the following materials in Interfolio : (1) a cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) research statement, (4) teaching and mentoring statement, and (5) a list of three references. Please see CAEE Faculy Openings for additional information and guidance. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Please address questions to Prof. Charles J. Werth, CAEE Department Chair ( werth@utexas.edu ). Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026 , and will continue until an appropriate candidate is identified. Further information on the Maseeh Department of Civil, Architectural, and Environmental Engineering may be found at the department webpage . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Civil, Architectural & Environmental Engineering Posted Oct 8, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
