    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193145": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Experimental Particle Physics",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Assistant Professor in Experimental Particle Physics Apply now Description The Department of Physics at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level by particle physics experimentalists working on non-collider searches and studies of rare phenomena including neutrino oscillations, neutrinoless double beta decay, and direct dark matter detection. We seek applications from candidates with demonstrated outstanding track record of professional accomplishments. Candidates are expected to lead vigorous forefront research, be committed to innovative and engaging teaching, and participate in broad community services.  Our department offers excellent start-up funding, salary, and where applicable, laboratory space in a dynamic and highly interactive research environment. Qualifications Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Physics or equivalent experience. Application Instructions Please submit your application for this position in experimental particle physics at apply.interfolio.com/194655 and include: 1) a cover letter, 2) a complete Curriculum Vitae, and a list of publications, 3) a research statement describing accomplishments and current and future research plans (limited to 4 pages), 4) a teaching statement describing teaching experience, goals, and philosophy, Applicants must arrange for three letters of recommendations to be uploaded using the Interfolio website. For full consideration, all application materials should be received by 11:59p.m. CST on December 2, 2026.  Inquiries about the application process and status should be submitted to Physics Faculty Hiring at faculty.search@physics.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Physics Posted Sep 30, 2026 Apply by Dec 2, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192948": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Classics",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor of Classics Apply now Description The Department of Classics at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor to begin in the fall of academic year 2027-28. We seek a Romanist working in the Republican period, with specialization either in history (particular areas of interest include political, military, or social history) or language/literature (particular areas of interest include poetry, world literature, pedagogy, or critical theory). In addition to actively researching and publishing, responsibilities include providing administrative and committee service to the department, directing undergraduate honors theses and eventually Ph.D. dissertations, and teaching graduate and undergraduate courses in Classics. Duties will include contributing to the department's Latin instruction at all levels and teaching in the core curriculum (particularly in \"Western Civilization\"). Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the Ph.D. by time of appointment, or they must expect to obtain the Ph.D. within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. Application Instructions To apply, upload a letter of interest, CV, three letters of recommendation, teaching portfolio, and writing samples. Applications must be received no later than November 1, 2026. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Positiong funding is subject to budget availability. For further information, please contact: Professor Andrew Riggsby, Search Committee Chair Email: ariggsby@utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Classics Posted Sep 30, 2026 Apply by Nov 1, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193102": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (TT)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor (TT) – History of Science, Technology, and Medicine Apply now Description The Department of History at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor to begin Fall of the academic year 2027-28. We seek a historian specializing in the modern history of science, technology, and medicine. The search committee will consider applicants who specialize in any scientific practice in any part of the world since 1600. Duties will include undergraduate and graduate teaching, research, and service to the department, college, and university. The successful candidate must be willing and able to teach in the core curriculum (i.e. “Great Ideas in Science”). Opportunities for collaboration and engagement include the new History+Computer Science major; the History and Philosophy of Science Certificate Program; the Health and Society major; the Humanities, Health and Medicine graduate program; the Dell Medical School; and the Science, Technology, and Environment track in the International Relations and Global Studies major. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the Ph.D. by time of appointment, or they must expect to obtain the Ph.D. within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. We require graduate training in conducting historical research and a Ph.D. in History; the History of Science, Technology, and Medicine; Science and Technology Studies; or a related field. Preferred qualifications include research and teaching records that will complement one or more of the department’s strengths including: African History, Asian History, British History, Digital Humanities, Latin American History, U.S. History, and related fields. Application Instructions To apply, upload to Interfolio a cover letter detailing research and teaching interests, a CV, a 20-page writing sample, and a list of the names and email addresses of three recommenders . Additional materials may be requested after the initial review of applications. The committee will begin reviewing applications on November 6 and continue until the position is filled. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For further information, please contact: Prof. Abena Osseo-Asare, Search Committee Chair osseo@utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of History Posted Sep 29, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193081": {
      "title": "Professional-Track Assistant Professor Position in AI and Journalism",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Moody College of Communication Professional-Track Assistant Professor Position in AI and Journalism Apply now Description The School of Journalism and Media at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a full-time, professional-track faculty member at the rank of assistant professor in the areas of AI, journalism and media to begin in Fall 2027. The position is a three-year appointment. The School is part of the top-ranked Moody College of Communication, which includes programs in Advertising & Public Relations, Communication and Leadership, Communication Studies, Radio-Television-Film, and Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences. The School, which offers B.J., M.A., and Ph.D. degrees, is housed in the G.B. Dealey Center for New Media. We seek an accomplished media professional whose work centers on the integration of artificial intelligence into journalism and media. Individuals with backgrounds in technology reporting, data journalism, applications of Ai in the practice of journalism, and/or AI-related product design and development are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses in AI, journalism and media and, depending on curricular needs, may also teach courses that serve students across Moody College's majors. Dependent upon their expertise, the candidate may be asked to teach topics including the ethics of AI use, reporting and writing with AI, the impact of AI on journalism and media, and other uses of AI in newsrooms and other media-focused workplaces. The faculty member will mentor students, contribute to curriculum development, help strengthen relationships with industry partners, and support the school’s and college’s efforts to assess how students are learning to apply AI tools, identify gaps in preparedness and inform curricular strategy over time . Dependent upon the qualifications of the candidate, there may be opportunities to participate in the school’s Media Innovation Group, an important organization funded by the Dallas Morning News Initiative. School of Journalism and Media: https://journalism.utexas.edu/ Moody College of Communication: http://moody.utexas.edu/ Qualifications Applicants must hold a bachelor’s degree in journalism or a related field and have at least five years of professional media experience at the time of appointment. Experience teaching at the college level is preferred. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a letter of interest, resume/curriculum vitae, and an online portfolio showcasing recent professional work. Please also provide the names and contact information for three references. Letters of recommendation will be requested of finalists. Screening of applicants will begin November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must be made via Interfolio. If you do not have a Dossier account with Interfolio, you will be prompted to create one prior to applying for the position. If you have questions about using Interfolio, please email help@interfolio.com or call (877) 997-8807. Questions can be directed to the chair of the search committee, Professor S. Craig Watkins at craig.watkins@austin.utexas.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Moody College of Communication Department School of Journalism and Media Posted Sep 29, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/193080": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in French and Francophone Studies",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor in French and Francophone Studies Apply now Description The Department of European and Eurasian Studies at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of French and Francophone Studies to begin Fall 2027. We seek an interdisciplinary scholar working at the intersection of language and culture from transnational perspectives (specialization open). Duties include teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, developing courses for the department, service at the departmental, college, and university levels, and collaboration with colleagues and students from allied programs on campus. The successful candidate must also be willing and able to teach in the university's core curriculum. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all requirements for the Ph.D. by the time of appointment or expect to obtain the Ph.D within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. Native-or-near native fluency in French and English required. Experience in course development, student mentoring, and collaborative projects is required. Preferred qualifications include proficiency in additional research languages as well as a strong track record of teaching excellence and scholarly accomplishments (articles, monographs) beyond the dissertation. Scholarly agendas focused on historical and contemporary issues in the Francophone world (Africa, North America, Europe, Pacific, Caribbean) and on varities of French in these cultural and linguistic contact zones are preferred, with preferred subfields including visual arts, anthropology, intellectual and political history, linguistics, philosophy, translation studies, Digital Humanities, as well as other approaches to text, performance, and material cultures. Application Instructions Please upload a detailed letter of interest, CV, names and contact information for three recommenders, teaching portfolio (including at least three sets of teaching evaluations), and a writing sample (scholarly article or dissertation chapter). Additional materials may be requested after the initial review of applications. Applications must be received no later than November 2, 2026. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For further information, please contact: David Birdsong, Search Committee Chair, at birdsong@austin.utexas.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of European and Eurasian Studies Posted Sep 29, 2026 Apply by Nov 2, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192893": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track and Tenured Faculty Positions in Electrical and Computer Engineering, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Tenure-Track and Tenured Faculty Positions in Electrical and Computer Engineering, Fall 2027 Apply now Description The Chandra Family Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at The University of Texas Austin has multiple faculty openings with a start date of Fall 2027 for tenure-track and tenured faculty positions . Candidates in all areas of electrical and computer engineering will be considered for tenure-track positions only, unless explicitly stated otherwise, especially as they align with the strategic focus of the department in: (i) Core/foundational AI; (ii) computer systems (tenured faculty candidates will also be considered for this area); and (iii) robotics and physical AI. We are particularly interested in candidates who will contribute to supporting our student body through their teaching, research, and service. Successful candidates are also expected to contribute to and benefit from the department’s mission and values: be the driving force for education enabling individual student success, scientific discovery, and technical innovation in the service of social good; and be bold, innovative, and collaborative in their aims and endeavors, while valuing, appreciating, and welcoming individuals of all backgrounds and worldviews. The Chandra Family Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at the University of Texas at Austin is also committed to addressing the family needs of faculty, including dual-career couples, families with children, and single parents. Qualifications Applicants should have received or expect to receive a doctoral degree in electrical engineering, computer engineering, computer science, or a related discipline prior to August 2027. Successful candidates are expected to mentor graduate students, teach our undergraduate and graduate students, develop an externally sponsored research program, collaborate with other faculty in a wide range of fields, and be involved in service to the university and profession. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted electronically via UT Austiin Interfolio.  We will begin evaluation of applications immediately and will continue throughout the academic year until positions are filled. Submission of applications by October 15, 2026 , is strongly encouraged. Applications received after November 15, 2026 may not be given equal priority. Interested applicants should submit a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, statement of research, statement of teaching philosophy, and a statement describing their mentoring philosophy in both research and teaching. At least three references (five for tenured positions) who will submit original letters of reference should be included. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the U.S. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. The University of Texas at Austin is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. Suggestions for application statements Research statement: Describe your research contributions to date, and your short and long-term plans for your research program. An effective research statement will highlight your individual contributions and describe past and potential future collaborations with others, both within the department and UT, as well as outside the campus. Please also highlight the impact of your research and describe any potential applications that benefit society. Teaching statement: Describe your teaching experience to date, your teaching philosophy, and your plans for teaching. Please identify undergraduate and/or graduate courses that you could teach, indicating for each your experience with the material and your interest and approach to teaching it. An effective teaching statement will describe your pedagogical approach in the classroom and convey how your prior experiences shape your teaching plans. Mentoring statement: Describe your mentoring experience to date and your mentoring philosophy in both teaching and research. Please highlight your experience with or interest in mentoring and advising undergraduate and graduate students, including how you plan to support each individual student's academic success, professional development, educational growth, and the ethical conduct of research and scholarship. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Electrical & Computer Engineering Posted Sep 29, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192895": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Instructional Faculty Positions (Open Rank) Apply now Description The Department of Computer Science of the University of Texas at Austin is recruiting for full-time, professional track faculty (Lecturer, Assistant Professor of Instruction, Associate Professor of Instruction, Professor of Instruction) positions. We encourage applications from candidates who will join our welcoming community and contribute to it through excellence in teaching and other contributions to the academic enterprise. The department is consistently ranked among the top computer science programs in the nation. As of Fall 2026, Computer Science is one of three academic units in UT Austin’s new School of Computing, alongside Statistics and Data Sciences and Information. The new School is designed to strengthen collaboration and create new opportunities for cross-departmental research and education. We are renowned for our collegial environment that fosters innovation and collaboration—an environment that attracts many top-notch faculty and excellent students from across Texas, the country, and the world. We value undergraduate education and have a committed group of highly effective educators, who are committed to helping all students reach the high standards, both technical and holistic, expected of our graduates. Our instructional faculty typically teach three courses a semester, with two of those courses usually being instances of the same course. Our class sizes tend to be on the smaller side of most of our peer departments: in Fall 2024, each faculty member taught an average of 230 students across three courses. The Department is responsive to the needs of dual career couples and supports work-life balance through an array of family-friendly policies. For more information about the department, please visit https://cs.utexas.edu . Austin, the capital of Texas, is a major technology hub, home to significant operations from companies such as Amazon, AMD, Apple, Applied Materials, AT&T, Dell, Google, IBM, Meta, NVIDIA, Samsung, Silicon Labs, and Tesla. Nestled at the edge of the Texas Hill Country, much of Austin’s lifestyle is driven by outdoor activities, media, and music, due to the availability of green space, the presence of Lady Bird Lake (a consequence of a dammed river) running through town, and the location of events such as SxSW and ACL Music Festival, to name a select few.  Check out our local public radio station KUT’s Field Guide to Austin for a wide variety of Austin topics or the Austin Visitor Center’s site for information on things to do. Position Description For this position, the primary duty is teaching undergraduate courses, which includes a wide variety of opportunities, though teaching in our in-person and online graduate programs may be possible for some candidates. While these positions are not eligible for tenure, they have opportunities for advancement, and we hire with the intent that new instructional faculty will become a lasting part of our community. Teaching Opportunities Teaching opportunities range from introductory computer science courses, such as data structures, discrete mathematics, and computer architecture, to advanced courses, particularly topics related to artificial intelligence, computer systems, and UI/UX. We have opportunities to teach classes dedicated to CS majors as well as classes targeted to majors from other disciplines—many of our instructional faculty teach both types of classes. For the 2026-2027 cycle, we are particularly interested in candidates that can teach artificial intelligence and related areas, as well as introductory computer science topics. Our curriculum offers students an opportunity to investigate the social impact of computer science, and so we have opportunities to develop and teach classes that are technical with social impact considerations integrated into the content as well as stand alone technical courses and those related to social impact and ethics. Finally, we are launching interdisciplinary degrees, and so we will have opportunities to teach capstone courses in the integrated disciplines. In Fall 2025, we welcomed our first students in our History+CS, Linguistics+CS, and Neuroscience+CS programs. In our department, faculty teaching large courses are supported by Graduate Teaching Assistants (TA), Undergraduate Course Assistants (UGCA), or a combination of the two. We find that our TAs and UGCAs are motivated and enjoy helping students, and many of our instructional faculty develop strong collaborations and close mentoring relationships with their TAs and UGCAs. As part of the position, faculty who are assigned TAs or UGCAs are responsible for assigning and overseeing their duties. Applicants able to teach any of the aforementioned topics are encouraged to apply. Our faculty work together to provide the courses the students need. We expect deep expertise in each faculty member’s subject area and value a willingness to teach related topics or introductory courses as course needs change. Additional Contributions to the Academic Enterprise In addition to teaching, instructional faculty at UT Austin are also expected to contribute to the academic enterprise through active service, research, mentoring, or any combination of the three. Each faculty member creates their own unique set of contributions based on interest, skill sets, and need, but we do expect our instructional faculty to spend some of their time encouraging and mentoring students towards careers in industry, government, and the academy, as well as developing and improving the curriculum within their courses. Instructional faculty have the opportunity to contribute through committee service, too, as they serve on and chair committees along with other faculty in the department. Qualifications ● A Master’s degree in Computer Science or related discipline, including a depth of knowledge in the expected teaching areas is required. A Ph.D. in Computer Science or a related discipline is preferred. ● Strong communication skills are necessary, particularly an ability to clearly communicate complicated technical concepts ● A willingness to collaborate and foster community is required, as we are a collegial and collaborative department, and we hope you will join us in our mission to maintain that environment. ● Previous teaching experience is not required, but it is helpful. Application Instructions All faculty positions require a cover letter, current curriculum vita, teaching statement and three (3) professional reference letters. Letters of reference must address the candidate’s overall quality of teaching and/or presentation skills, the ability to communicate complex topics, and expertise in the field of computer science. As part of their teaching statement, applicants are encouraged to include examples of effective pedagogy they use or plan to use in their classroom, including how they engage or plan to engage students. This description may include activities or strategies they employ or plan to employ. For applicants with teaching experience, please include sample course materials and assignments; peer or student evaluations; and any descriptions of pedagogical innovations or continuous improvements that have been employed in your courses. Please submit those materials as a single file with your teaching statement. Applicants who desire confidentiality should explicitly mention this in the first paragraph of their cover letter. We will consider applications in two rounds, with deadlines of November 15, 2026, and February 1, 2027. Any applications received after those dates may be considered until the positions are filled. The expected start date is Fall 2027, though other possibilities will be considered. Inquiries may be directed to faculty-search@cs.utexas.edu . The University of Texas is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Computer Science Posted Sep 28, 2026 Apply by May 31, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192902": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Instructor",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Postdoctoral Instructor - R.H. Bing Fellowship 2027 Apply now Description The Department of Mathematics is expecting one to three opening(s) for an Instructorships associated with R.H. Bing Faculty Fellowship for fall 2027. Excellence in research and teaching is at the core of our mission. The graduate program of the Department of Mathematics is consistently ranked among the best by US News, with several research areas in the top ten. Our core faculty includes around 50 tenured and tenure-track members, many of whom have attracted top national and international distinctions. Moreover, we are home to a talented group of professional track and postdoctoral faculty, and a large and diverse population of highly motivated and talented graduate students. Strengths in our research program include many areas in pure and applied mathematics, as well as interdisciplinary fields intersecting engineering, neuroscience, computer science, medicine, finance, and natural sciences, among others. As the flagship public university in Texas, a core part of our mission is the education of undergraduate students from all over this large and varied state, in a supportive environment. Austin, the capital of Texas, is well known for its high quality of living, its thriving economy, and for being a center for high-technology industry, including companies such as Amazon, AMD, Apple, Applied Materials, AT&T, Dell, Google, IBM, National Instruments, and Samsung. For more information about the department, please visit the Department of Mathematics. https://www.ma.utexas.edu/ Each R.H. Bing Fellow holds an Instructorship in the Department of Mathematics with a teaching load of one course per long semester. The combined Instructorship-Fellowship stipend for nine months is $60,000, which is supplemented by an annual research allowance of $2,000. Pending satisfactory performance of teaching duties, the Fellowship can be renewed for two additional years. Applicants must show outstanding promise in research. Bing Fellowship applicants will automatically be considered for other departmental openings at the postdoctoral level, so a separate application for such a position is unnecessary. Applications will be considered on a continuing basis with priority given to complete applications received by November 30, 2026 . Applications should be supported by three or more letters of recommendation, at least one of which speaks to the applicant's teaching credentials. The screening of applications will begin on December 1, 2026. Qualifications Ph.D. by July 1, 2027. Application Instructions Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application via https://apply.interfolio.com/194468 : Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writer on this site) Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Mathematics Posted Sep 25, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192822": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Medieval History",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor of Medieval History Apply now Description The Department of History at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor to begin in Fall 2027. We seek a scholar to teach medieval Europe, excluding Spain, with additional expertise in premodern global interconnections, especially the contacts between cultures and societies along the overland or maritime Silk Road(s) that encompass Eurasia, 500-1500 CE, including China. We also encourage applications from specialists in diasporic medieval populations, including global Jewish communities beyond the Mediterranean. The successful candidate must be willing and able to teach in the core curriculum (particularly in the “Western Civilization” core area). Duties will include undergraduate and graduate teaching, research, and service to the department, college, and university. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the Ph.D. by time of appointment, or they must expect to obtain the Ph.D. within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. Some teaching experience in European medieval history, Western civilization, Silk Road, Asia, and/or diasporic medieval populations is required. Application Instructions To apply, upload a statement of interest, teaching statement, CV, three letters of recommendation, two relevant syllabi, and writing sample. Additional materials may be requested after initial review of applications. Applications must be received no later than November 13, 2026. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For further information, please contact: Denise A. Spellberg, Search Committee Chair spellberg@austin.utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of History Posted Sep 24, 2026 Apply by Nov 13, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192679": {
      "title": "Middle Eastern Studies - Assistant Professor of Instruction (Non-Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Department of Middle Eastern Studies Middle Eastern Studies - Assistant Professor of Instruction (Non-Tenure Track) Apply now Description The Department of Middle Eastern Studies at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a professional-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor of Instruction. The position will begin in the Fall semester of the 2027–28 academic year. Duties will include teaching Hebrew as a foreign language at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Additional teaching responsibilities may be required, such as courses on Hebrew/Israeli culture and undergraduate Hebrew language content courses. We seek candidates interested in a career devoted to teaching and general service to the university. Professional-track faculty are encouraged to fully integrate into the university’s faculty and intellectual life, with opportunities for advancement through promotion and multi-year contracts. Qualifications A Ph.D. in Middle Eastern Languages and Cultures, Comparative Literature, Jewish Studies, or a related field and two years of experience teaching Hebrew or Hebrew Literature are required. Preferred qualifications include training in comparative literature and at least one year of experience teaching Yiddish. Application Instructions To apply, candidates should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, sample syllabi (both graduate and undergraduate), and the names of three references who could provide letters of recommendation at a later stage. Additional materials may be requested after initial review of applications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For further information, please contact: Bruce Wells Department Chair bruce.wells@austin.utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Department of Middle Eastern Studies Posted Sep 24, 2026 Apply by Oct 31, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192597": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Curriculum and Instruction with a Specialization in Learning Technologies",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Associate or Full Professor of Curriculum and Instruction with a Specialization in Learning Technologies Apply now Description The Department of Curriculum and Instruction in the College of Education at The University of Texas at Austin seeks a faculty member at the associate professor or full professor level whose scholarship advances Learning Technologies. Although the primary appointment falls within the Learning Technologies program area in the Department of Curriculum and Instruction, the successful candidate will have the ability to lead college-wide initiatives related to Learning Technologies. A secondary appointment in the Department of Kinesiology and Health Education, Special Education, Educational Psychology, or Educational Leadership and Policy will also be considered. The successful candidate will have a strong record of research focusing on the applications, implications, and innovations of Learning Technologies in EC–12, higher education, professional learning, or informal contexts. Such scholarship may include, but is not limited to, studies of generative AI in teaching and learning; learning analytics and educational data science; human-AI interaction; the design of AI- and technology-enriched learning environments; technology-supported curriculum design and assessment; technology-rich teacher education and professional learning; and accessible, culturally responsive, and community-engaged learning technologies. We welcome scholarship that advances educational innovations while critically examining the pedagogical, ethical, social, cultural, and policy implications of AI and emerging technologies. We especially welcome applicants whose work reimagines the future of education through responsible uses of AI and digital technologies while remaining attentive to the diverse contexts in which teaching and learning occur. The successful candidate will have a record of securing external grants to support a research program with meaningful impact. The successful candidate will teach and mentor undergraduate students and graduate students. In Learning Technologies, a graduate program, they may teach courses in areas such as instructional design, learning theory, research methods, design-based research, online and blended learning, educational data and AI, technology-supported curriculum and instruction, and teacher education. The successful candidate will also show interest in participating in existing, or creating new, interdisciplinary collaborations that explore innovative and responsible uses of AI and Learning Technologies. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students’ diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and many other academic and research units on campus. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Qualifications Earned doctoral degree in Learning Technologies, Curriculum and Instruction, education, learning sciences, educational technology, instructional design, or a related field. Record of research that examines the applications, implications, or innovations in Learning Technologies within EC–12, higher education, professional learning, informal, or out-of-school contexts. Record of scholarly leadership, including high-impact peer-reviewed publications and external research funding appropriate to the candidate’s career stage. Record of teaching and mentoring graduate students, including chairing or serving on dissertation committees and guiding doctoral research projects. Interest in collaboration with faculty in Learning Technologies, other program areas in Curriculum and Instruction, the College of Education, and interdisciplinary partners across UT Austin. Application Instructions Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, candidates are encouraged to apply by November 30, 2026. Applications should be submitted through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/194193 and include the following materials: Cover Letter with contact information Full C.V. Research Statement that includes research accomplishments and future research direction Teaching and Mentoring Statement that describes specific methods used to ensure effective student mentoring and a classroom environment that is welcoming, engaging, and supportive of student learning and success Up to three representative publications A list of three references with contact information For additional information about the Department of Curriculum and Instruction, visit our website . For questions regarding the application process, please contact Jim Maxwell . Questions about the position can be directed to a co-chair of the search committee, Dr. Cinthia Salinas . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Curriculum & Instruction Posted Sep 24, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192547": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Curriculum and Instruction with a Specialization in Learning Technologies",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Assistant Professor of Curriculum and Instruction with a Specialization in Learning Technologies Apply now Description The Department of Curriculum and Instruction in the College of Education at The University of Texas at Austin seeks a faculty member at the assistant professor (tenure-track) level whose scholarship advances Learning Technologies in the areas of EC–12 education and teacher education. We seek a scholar whose research examines the applications, policies, innovations and implications of learning technologies within EC–12 or teacher education contexts. Such scholarship may include, but is not limited to, studies that examine classroom-based applications of technology; youth and educators’ uses of generative AI and emerging technologies; and the use of generative AI to reimagine the nature or process of curriculum design, assessment, or teaching. We welcome applicants whose scholarship reimagines the future of education through generative AI and emerging technologies while remaining grounded in the lived experiences, cultural knowledge, and epistemologies of learners and communities. We are particularly interested in candidates who are engaged in preparing the next generation of teachers who will be able to use a range of AI and emerging technology tools to create learning experiences that are meaningful for all learners. Strong applicants will demonstrate potential for or evidence of a significant research program that contributes to the field of Learning Technologies and has implications for educators, learners, schools, community organizations, or higher education. The successful candidate will be prepared to secure external grants to support a research program with meaningful impact. The successful candidate will teach and mentor undergraduate students and graduate students. In Learning Technologies, a graduate program, they may teach courses in areas such as instructional design, learning theory, research methods, design-based research, online and blended learning, educational data and AI, technology-supported curriculum and instruction, and teacher education. The successful candidate will also show interest in participating in existing, or creating new, interdisciplinary collaborations that explore innovative and responsible uses of AI and Learning Technologies. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students’ diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and many other academic and research units on campus. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Qualifications Required Qualifications Doctoral degree in Learning Technologies, Curriculum and Instruction, education, learning sciences, educational technology, instructional design, or a related field earned by July 31, 2027 Potential for or record of research that examines the applications, implications, or innovations in AI and emerging technologies within EC–12 or teacher education contexts Potential for or record of scholarly productivity (i.e., refereed publications, external funding) Potential for or record of teaching and mentoring graduate students through chairing dissertation committees and guiding doctoral research projects Preferred Qualifications Teaching experience in EC–12 settings and/or in teacher preparation program courses Application Instructions Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, candidates are encouraged to apply by November 30, 2026. All applications should be submitted through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/194150 and include the following materials: Cover Letter with contact information Full C.V. Research Statement that includes research accomplishments and future research direction Teaching and Mentoring Statement that describes specific methods used to ensure effective student mentoring and a classroom environment that is welcoming, engaging, and supportive of student learning and success. Up to three representative publications A list of three references with contact information For additional information about the Department of Curriculum and Instruction, visit our website . For questions regarding the application process, please contact Jim Maxwell . Questions about the position can be directed to a co-chair of the search committee, Dr. Anthony Brown . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Curriculum & Instruction Posted Sep 24, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192733": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor - Public Law Apply now Description The Department of Government at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track position in Public Law at the rank of Assistant Professor. The search is open to candidates whose research engages substantially with law and legal institutions, including scholars working also in American Politics, Comparative Politics, International Relations, Political Theory, or Political Methodology. Duties will include undergraduate and graduate teaching, research, and service to the Department, the College of Liberal Arts, and the University, including teaching in the Core Curriculum (particularly “Principles of American Politics” and/or “World Politics and Culture”). UT Government is one of the largest political science departments in the country. With nearly 60 faculty covering almost every facet of the discipline and a strong commitment to eclecticism in theoretical and methodological approaches, it is a remarkable and remarkably collegial intellectual community. Located in Austin, at Texas’ flagship public university, we also have the privilege of serving a plural and gifted student population, in a vibrant and dynamic city. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the Ph.D. by the time of appointment or expect to obtain the Ph.D. within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. Preferred qualifications include a strong record of research and a trajectory that shows or promises teaching excellence. Application Instructions Interested candidates should upload a cover letter, vitae, research statement, examples of scholarly work, teaching portfolio, graduate transcripts, and three letters of recommendation. The department will begin reviewing applications on October 12, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. For help with the application process please reach out to Sarah Eliason ( seliason@austin.utexas.edu ). For substantive questions about the position , please contact: Zachary Elkins, Search Committee Chair ( zelkins@austin.utexas.edu ). Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Government Posted Sep 23, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192729": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor - Comparative Politics Apply now Description The Department of Government at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track position in Comparative Politics at the rank of Assistant Professor. The search is open to scholars working in Africa and Asia with particular preference for those working in South Asia. Duties will include undergraduate and graduate teaching, research, and service to the Department, the College of Liberal Arts, and the University, including teaching in the Core Curriculum (particularly “World Politics and Cultures” and/or “Principles of American Politics”). UT Government is one of the largest political science departments in the country. With nearly 60 faculty covering almost every facet of the discipline and a strong commitment to eclecticism in theoretical and methodological approaches, it is a remarkable and remarkably collegial intellectual community. Located in Austin, at Texas’ flagship public university, we also have the privilege of serving a plural and gifted student population, in a vibrant and dynamic city. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the Ph.D. by the time of appointment or expect to obtain the Ph.D. within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. Preferred qualifications include a strong record of research and a trajectory that shows or promises teaching excellence. Application Instructions Interested candidates should upload a cover letter, vitae, research statement, examples of scholarly work, teaching portfolio, graduate transcripts, and three letters of recommendation. The department will begin reviewing applications on October 12, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. For help with the application process please reach out to Sarah Eliason ( seliason@austin.utexas.edu ). For substantive questions about the position , please contact: Patricia Maclachlan, Search Committee Chair ( pmaclachlan@austin.utexas.edu ). Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Government Posted Sep 23, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192625": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Position in Artificial Intelligence and Operations Management",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions McCombs School of Business Tenure Track Position in Artificial Intelligence and Operations Management Apply now Description The McCombs School of Business at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a faculty position in Artificial Intelligence and Operations Management, beginning in Fall 2027. We seek candidates with a strong commitment to high-quality research and teaching who can contribute to our undergraduate and graduate courses in Artificial Intelligence and Operations Management. We are particularly interested in candidates who use rigorous empirical and data-driven methods to address problems in application domains such as revenue management, supply chains, healthcare, retailing, transportation and logistics, and technology and operations. A successful candidate will be expected to establish an internationally recognized research program at The University of Texas at Austin, mentor graduate students, and teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The predominant mode of instruction and research is in person; accordingly, faculty appointed for employment shall reside in the proximate area of the work site for the duration of their appointment and be able to maintain a daily in-person schedule at the assigned work site. The McCombs School of Business, with its top-ranked faculty and educational programs, offers a stimulating and collegial environment for innovative research and teaching. The school’s research centers, including centers focused on supply chain management, electronic commerce, entrepreneurship, marketing, and energy management, provide opportunities to collaborate with colleagues across disciplines and interact with a range of industry partners in Austin, throughout Texas, and nationally. A background check will be conducted on applicants selected for the position prior to appointment. Qualifications A Ph.D. (or very near completion) from an accredited institution is required by the start date of the appointment. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, a job-market paper or other representative research manuscript, and a statement of research objectives and accomplishments. Three confidential letters of recommendation should also be submitted via Interfolio. Candidates with teaching experience should upload teaching evaluations. At their discretion, applicants with prior faculty experience, such as current assistant or associate professors, may defer submission of recommendation letters during the initial application stage. Such candidates will be asked to provide references if selected as finalists. Additional materials may also be requested after the initial review of applications. All materials should be submitted through Interfolio using the following link: apply.interfolio.com/194218 The application deadline is December 1, 2026. Applications received by October 15, 2026, will also be considered for interviews at the INFORMS Annual Meeting on November 1, 2026. Please do not submit applications or updated materials via email. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit McCombs School of Business Department Department of Information, Risk, & Operations Management (IROM) Posted Sep 23, 2026 Apply by Dec 1, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192600": {
      "title": "Associate Chair of Investigation and Discovery",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Dell Medical School Associate Chair of Investigation and Discovery Apply now Description About Dell Medical School As part of The University of Texas at Austin, one of the nation’s leading research universities, the Dell Medical School pursues innovation in the redesign of healthcare delivery, excellence in healthcare research, and programs in interdisciplinary and inter-professional education. It is the fifth medical school in the UT System and the first medical school established in nearly five decades by a member of the Association of American Universities, an organization of leading public and private research universities. The University of Texas Medical Center, anchored by Dell Medical School, is defining the future of health. Announced in August 2023 with planning and implementation underway, The University of Texas Medical Center will cement Austin as a premier destination for health care alongside other major Texas cities. With two new, state-of-the-art hospitals – A UT specialty hospital and a cancer center being built by MD Anderson – plus the academic and research expertise of UT, The University of Texas Medical Center will provide the integrated, world-class care that is a hallmark of academic medicine. The Dell Medical School is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to excellence within the learning health system. About The Associate Chair of Investigation and Discover The Dell Medical School of The University of Texas at Austin is seeking an exceptional leader to serve as the Associate Chair of Investigation and Discovery of the Department of Women’s Health. The Associate Chair of Investigation and Discovery will be an integral part of the continued growth and development of the research program within the Department.  The position is sub-specialty agnostic, thus open to applicants independent of area of sub-specialization or interest. Specific job responsibilities will include: · Partner with Department leadership to develop a strategic plan for research; aligning with the clinical priorities of the Department and building on the existing areas of strength within the myriad of outstanding programs at UT Austin · Lead the continued growth and development of a sustainable research infrastructure for the Department including an existing research support team of eleven · Mentor junior faculty, staff and learners · Create models for faculty and trainee research support, including training, tools and collaborative platforms · Identify and secure funding for Departmental research priorities · Enhance opportunities for implementation and dissemination of discovery to improve health in the Austin community · Develop metrics and performance evaluation processes for faculty members’ research contributions in conjunction with Department Chair · Collaborate with the Associate Chair of Clinical Integration and Operations and Education to ensure cohesive, mutually reinforcing initiatives · Represent the Department in key research related decision making bodies within the Dell Medical School and with care partners · Collaborate with other departmental Associate Chairs of Investigation and Discovery in other Dell Medical School Departments on cross-cutting initiatives · Develop and foster cross-campus collaborations · Support the continued development of an innovative research curriculum in graduate medical education Live in Austin a Vibrant, Lively, University City Known for its excellent schools, lively music scene, superb cuisine, and international festivals such as South by Southwest. The city typically ranks among the country’s best places to live. Nearby lakes and rivers as well as the beautiful adjacent Texas Hill Country provide ample opportunities for outdoor activities. Why Join Our Team We offer an attractive compensation and benefits package that is well rounded and competitive, making it desirable for individuals with excellent clinical skills, a passion for education, and potential for scholarly contributions to join a dynamic department in an innovative and growing medical school. Qualifications Required qualifications: ·       Medical degree from an accredited medical school ·       Board certification in obstetrics and gynecology ·       Eligible for appointment as a tenured associate professor or professor ·       Substantial record of leadership and achievement in academic medicine including sustained acquisition and participation in funded work ·       Experience in leading large-scale research programs in obstetrics and gynecology Preferred qualifications: ·       Demonstrated track record of innovation in research and integration into clinical practice ·       Demonstrated record of facilitating cross-disciplinary connections to advance research priorities #healthstartshere1 Application Instructions Interested and qualified candidates should complete the required application and submit a current CV. #healthstartshere1 Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Dell Medical School Department Department of Women's Health Posted Sep 22, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192571": {
      "title": "AY 2026-27 Professional-Track Faculty Position in Fine Arts",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Fine Arts AY 2026-27 Professional-Track Faculty Position in Fine Arts Apply now Description The College of Fine Arts offers a unique opportunity for professional development in teaching, creative practice and research. Positions are available periodically for non-tenure track faculty who are interested in teaching and who have the potential to contribute to the development of academic programs and creative activity in all areas of Fine Arts, particularly arts administration. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent on funding. This is a temporary/adjunct/contingent faculty position and is non-tenure track. Anticipated starting dates may be either mid August or beginning of January. The University of Texas at Austin and the College of Fine Arts is committed to fostering a welcoming community. We welcome applicants who can help achieve this objective. Qualifications Qualifications must be commensurate with rank and include an outstanding academic record, significant achievement in original research/creative activity/exhibition record and a commitment to quality teaching.  The successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate courses, graduate courses, collaborate with other faculty, and be involved in professional/creative research. Application Instructions Interested candidates are invited to upload 1) a letter of application, 2) a curriculum vita, and 3) one document with names and contact information for three references.  All materials should be submitted by clicking the \"Apply Now for Free\" button, below.  Applications will be accepted throughout the academic year.  Please do not send other supporting material unless requested. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Fine Arts Department Fine Arts Posted Sep 22, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192569": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Linguistics",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant or Associate Professor of Linguistics Apply now Description The Department of Linguistics at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor or Associate Professor with tenure to begin Fall of academic year 2027-2028. We seek candidates with a primary teaching and research specialization in the area of semantics and pragmatics. We are particularly interested in candidates who can interface with any of our other major areas of research in syntax, sounds, psycholinguistics, cognitive science, computational linguistics, language documentation, historical linguistics, sign language linguistics, or sociolinguistics. Duties will include research, service, and teaching linguistics courses at all levels (lower- and upper-division undergraduate, and graduate) and training the next generation of researchers working on semantics and pragmatics. Qualifications Applicants at the Assistant Professor level must have completed all the requirements for the PhD in linguistics, cognitive science, or related subfield by time of appointment. A PhD in linguistics, cognitive science, or related subfield is required for consideration at the rank of Associate Professor. Application Instructions Applicants must upload a letter of application, a CV, a statement describing their research program, a statement of teaching interests (including a list of courses that the applicant would be prepared to teach), evidence of past teaching performance or teaching potential, three letters of recommendation, and three writing samples. The search committee will begin reviewing applications on November 15th, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For further information, please contact: Prof. Stephen Wechsler, Search Committee Chair Email: wechsler@austin.utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Linguistics Posted Sep 22, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192544": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, tenure-track",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Assistant Professor, tenure-track Apply now Description The Department of Molecular Biosciences at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level in the field of biochemistry, with an emphasis on metabolism. We are particularly interested in collaborative and interdisciplinary scientists passionate about pursuing a mechanistic understanding of intermediary metabolism and metabolic regulation, elucidating connections with core biological processes or metabolic dysfunction, and developing innovative approaches to study cell metabolism. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, cellular and organellar metabolism, metabolic signaling, systems and computational approaches to metabolism, enzymology and metabolic regulation, structural and chemical biology of metabolic pathways, and the biochemical underpinnings of metabolic diseases (e.g., diabetes, obesity, tumor metabolism, and inborn errors of metabolism). The Department of Molecular Biosciences is composed of a broad faculty whose research interests span many disciplines including biochemistry, molecular biology, structural biology and biophysics, cell and developmental biology, plant biology, microbiology, immunology and infectious diseases, biotechnology, and systems and synthetic biology. We are excited to welcome collaborative scientists who actively interact with our faculty members and take advantage of our state-of-the-art facilities (see below). Successful candidates are expected to establish and sustain a vibrant research program; serve as dedicated teachers and mentors at both the undergraduate and graduate levels; and assist in the administrative and governance functions of the department, the university, and the profession via service on committees as appropriate. Applicants at the assistant professor level must have plans to develop an exciting independent research program and a strong commitment to graduate and undergraduate education. “What starts here changes the world.” UT Austin offers numerous resources and an exciting and interactive environment to foster creative interdisciplinary research. In addition to their research activities, our faculty are committed to the development of state-of-the art courses and real-world training experiences for undergraduate and graduate students as part of our core educational mission. Successful candidates will be eligible for membership in the Interdisciplinary Life Sciences Graduate Programs , which includes the Biochemistry Graduate Program , and collectively recruit a talented pool of graduate students each year. We also have outstanding core facilities, including those within the Center for Biomedical Research Support , the Texas Advanced Computing Center , and the Sauer Center for Structural Biology . The department has strong ties to other research units at UT Austin, including the School of Engineering, School of Pharmacy, Department of Computer Science, Oden Institute for Computational Engineering & Sciences , and Dell Medical School. This position offers excellent start-up funds, salary, and laboratory space in a dynamic and highly interactive research environment in the vibrant city of Austin. Applicants will also be encouraged to apply for support from the Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas ( CPRIT ). Qualifications Candidates must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in a relevant discipline and have an outstanding record of scientific ability as demonstrated by a strong publication record. Application Instructions Applicants should apply through Interfolio with the following documents: Cover letter that includes research area, qualifications, and contact information Curriculum vitae A statement of research interests (3-pages maximum) Three confidential letters of recommendation are required and will be requested through Interfolio Review of applications will begin on November 6, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Molecular Biosciences Posted Sep 22, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192532": {
      "title": "AY 2026-27 Non-Tenure Track Position in the Department of Design",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Fine Arts AY 2026-27 Non-Tenure Track Position in the Department of Design Apply now Description The Department of Design in the College of Fine Arts at The University of Texas at Austin offers a unique opportunity for professional development in teaching, creative practice, and research. Positions are available periodically for non-tenure track faculty who are interested in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in Design and other design-related fields. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent on funding. The University of Texas at Austin and the Department of Design are committed to fostering a welcoming community. We welcome applicants who can help achieve this objective. Qualifications Qualifications must be commensurate with rank and include an outstanding academic record, significant achievement in original research/creative activity/exhibition record and a commitment of quality teaching.  Successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses, collaborate with other faculty, and have the potential to be involved in professional/creative research. Application Instructions Interested applicants are invited to submit a letter of application, curriculum vita (with a live link to a portfolio, if applicable), and one document with names and contact information for three references. All materials should be submitted by clicking the \"Apply Now\" button.  Please do not submit additional supporting materials until requested. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Fine Arts Department Department of Design Posted Sep 22, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192530": {
      "title": "AY 2026-27 Non-Tenure Track Position in the Department of Arts and Entertainment Technologies",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Fine Arts AY 2026-27 Non-Tenure Track Position in the Department of Arts and Entertainment Technologies Apply now Description The Department of Arts and Entertainment Technologies in the College of Fine Arts at The University of Texas at Austin offers a unique opportunity for professional development in teaching, creative practice, and research. Positions are available periodically for non-tenure track faculty who are interested in teaching undergraduate courses in Arts and Entertainment Technologies and other technology-related fields. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent on funding. The University of Texas at Austin and the Department of Arts and Entertainment Technologies are committed to fostering a welcoming community. We welcome applicants who can help achieve this objective. Qualifications Qualifications must be commensurate with rank and include an outstanding academic record, significant achievement in original research/creative activity/exhibition record and a commitment of quality teaching.  Successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate courses, collaborate with other faculty, and have the potential to be involved in professional/creative research. Application Instructions Interested applicants are invited to submit a letter of application, curriculum vita (with a live link to a portfolio, if applicable), and one document with names and contact information for three references. All materials should be submitted by clicking the \"Apply Now\" button.  Please do not submit additional supporting materials until requested. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Fine Arts Department Department of Arts and Entertainment Technologies Posted Sep 22, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192523": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor (Tenured/Tenure-Track) in Computer Science",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Assistant, Associate, Full Professor (Tenured/Tenure-Track) in Computer Science Apply now Description The Department of Computer Science at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions. We are accepting applications from outstanding candidates in all areas of computer science. In addition to all core areas within CS, we encourage candidates with strong technical grounding in computer science whose work creates opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration, including potential partial or joint appointments in other departments at The University of Texas at Austin. As part of UT Austin’s School of Computing, Computer Science has close ties with the Department of Statistics and Data Sciences and the Department of Information, creating additional opportunities for joint appointments and cross-disciplinary research. We are also particularly interested in candidates advancing AI for Science, as well as those working specifically at the intersection of computer science with neuroscience, biology, or medicine. All interdisciplinary candidates are encouraged to send applications to all relevant departments for better university-wide coordination. Candidates working at the intersection of CS and Neuroscience are particularly encouraged to send applications to both departments to facilitate an explicit joint search. Candidates may also apply to the Neuroscience position via Interfolio ( apply.interfolio.com/193043 ). All positions are subject to the availability of funding. The department is ranked among the top computer science departments in the country. It has 64 tenured and tenure-track faculty members across all areas of computer science. Beginning in Fall 2026, Computer Science will be one of three founding academic units in UT Austin’s new School of Computing, alongside Statistics and Data Science and Information. The new School is designed to strengthen collaboration and create new opportunities for cross-departmental research and education. Many faculty also participate in interdisciplinary programs and centers across the University, including Texas Robotics, the Machine Learning Laboratory, the Institute on Foundations of Machine Learning (an NSF AI Institute), Learning Directed Operating Systems (NSF Expeditions in Computing), the Center for Generative AI, the Good Systems Grand Challenge on Ethical AI, the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC), and the Oden Institute for Computational Engineering and Science. Austin, the capital of Texas, is a major technology hub, home to significant operations from companies such as such as Amazon, AMD, Apple, Applied Materials, AT&T, Dell, Google, IBM, Meta, NVIDIA, Samsung, Silicon Labs, and Tesla. For more information about the department, please visit https://cs.utexas.edu . Qualifications All tenure-track positions require a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in computer science or a related area at the time of employment. Successful candidates are expected to pursue an active research program, to teach both graduate and undergraduate courses, and to supervise graduate students in research. Application Instructions All faculty positions require a cover letter, current curriculum vita, research statement, teaching statement and selected publications. For all positions, applicants must provide the names and contact information for at least three (3) references. Confidential letters of recommendation will be requested directly from the letter writers through Interfolio. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026. Please apply by then to ensure full consideration; however, the application system may remain open after that time. Applications received after the deadline are not guaranteed full consideration. Inquiries about your application may be directed to faculty-search@cs.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Computer Science Posted Sep 22, 2026 Apply by May 31, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192486": {
      "title": "Professor of Process Science and Technology",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Professor of Process Science and Technology - Chemical Engineering - Fall 2027 Apply now Description The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering at The University of Texas Austin is seeking to fill a senior faculty position, with a focus on industry-relevant research and teaching for reaction and/or separation processes and technologies at scale. Research focused on related machine learning and computational approaches, as well as modern high throughput experimentation platforms would also be well received. The ideal candidate will be a visionary leader who can bring together collaborative teams from academia and industry, advancing translational research that utilizes the University’s recent multi-million-dollar pilot-scale investments at the Process Science and Technology Center . Qualifications Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in chemical engineering or a closely related discipline with a record in academic research and experience with industry-academia partnerships. Appointments will be at the A ssociate/Full Professor, with tenure considered based on qualifications. Successful candidates are expected to mentor graduate students, teach our diverse undergraduate and graduate student populations, sustain a vibrant externally sponsored research program, collaborate with other faculty from a wide range of backgrounds, and be involved in service to the university and profession. The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering at University of Texas at Austin is committed to building a community that is welcoming to all and to addressing the family needs of faculty, including dual-career couples and single parents. Application Instructions Interested candidates should provide a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, statement of research interests, statement outlining plans for leading interdisciplinary research, mentoring statement, and statement of teaching philosophy and experience. Applicants must supply contact information for at least five references. The selected candidate will need to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documentation to verify identity and eligibility for U.S. employment. A security-sensitive background check will be performed for the final candidate. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Chemical Engineering Posted Sep 21, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192484": {
      "title": "Tenured Faculty Position in Chemical Engineering",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Tenured Faculty Position in Chemical Engineering - Fall 2027 Apply now Description The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering at The University of Texas Austin seeks outstanding applicants for a tenured appointment at the Associate or Full Professor rank, with a start date as early as Fall 2027. We seek candidates with demonstrated excellence in collaborative research in Chemical Engineering, and a strong interest in teaching and mentoring students and early-career scholars. Strategic areas of interest include catalysis, bioengineering, energy and materials, although e xcellent candidates in other areas will also be considered. Qualifications Tenured applicants must hold a Ph.D. in chemical engineering or a related discipline. Successful candidates are expected to mentor graduate students, teach our diverse undergraduate and graduate student populations, sustain a vibrant externally sponsored research program, collaborate with other faculty from a wide range of backgrounds, and be involved in service to the university and profession. The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering at University of Texas at Austin is committed to building a community that is welcoming to all and to addressing the family needs of faculty, including dual-career couples and single parents. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests, a mentoring statement, and statement of teaching philosophy and experience. At least five references who will submit original letters of reference should be included. The selected candidate will need to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documentation to verify identity and eligibility for U.S. employment. A security-sensitive background check will be performed for the final candidate. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Chemical Engineering Posted Sep 21, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192440": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology (Clinical)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor of Psychology (Clinical) Apply now Description The Department of Psychology at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Clinical Psychology to begin August 16, 2027. Our Clinical Psychology program is nationally recognized for high-quality research and clinical science training. Our current faculty conduct research that spans molecular biology to community implementation, with strengths in health psychology (including cognitive aging, eating pathology, and sexual health); anxiety, trauma, and exposure-based interventions; translational intervention science; and psychiatric genetics. We seek candidates whose research programs will complement and build on these strengths, with flexibility regarding the candidate’s specific field of specialization. The successful candidate will build an externally funded and nationally visible research program, while excelling as an instructor and mentor to doctoral students in our APA- and PCSAS-accredited Clinical Psychology Ph.D. program, and to undergraduate students. Department faculty have access to the Imaging Research Center and its Siemens Prisma 3T scanner, the Texas Advanced Computing Center (one of the country’s leading supercomputing centers), the clinical program’s in-house training clinic. Collaborative opportunities exist with departmental faculty in other areas, and with colleagues at Dell Medical School, the Waggoner Center for Alcohol and Addiction Research, Neuroscience, and the Population Research Center, among other units. This critical mass of expertise is well-situated to provide early-career mentorship and professional development opportunities. Duties will include developing an independent sponsored research program, collaborating with other faculty from a wide range of backgrounds, mentoring and teaching graduate and undergraduate students, contributing to clinical supervision and/or training, and serving the department, university, and profession. Qualifications Applicants must have: Completed all requirements for the Ph.D. in Clinical Psychology, or a closely related field, by the time of appointment A clearly defined research agenda A record of research excellence demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications Preferred qualifications include completion of an APA-accredited (or equivalent) predoctoral internship by time of appointment; potential for eligibility for licensure as a psychologist in Texas; a record of, or clear potential for, extramural research funding; and demonstrated commitment to teaching, mentoring, and/or supervision excellence. Application Instructions Interested candidates should upload a letter of interest (1 page), CV, research program statement (3 pages), teaching philosophy statement (1 page), up to 3 representative publications, and the names and contact information for 3–4 individuals who are willing to provide letters of recommendation. Additional materials may be requested after the initial review of applications. Review of applications will begin October 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For questions about the position, please contact Professor Andreana Haley, Search Committee Chair, haley@austin.utexas.edu. For administrative questions about the application, please contact Tessa Green, tessa.green@austin.utexas.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Psychology Posted Sep 21, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192432": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant Professor of Psychology Apply now Description The Department of Psychology at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position to begin August 16, 2027. We seek candidates whose work addresses enduring basic science questions of human behavior using cutting-edge methods, with expertise in social or developmental psychology and flexibility regarding the candidate’s specific field of specialization within those areas. Successful candidates will build an externally funded and nationally recognized research program and excel as instructors and mentors, joining vibrant and engaged colleagues from a range of scholarly backgrounds who seek to study behavior across the lifespan, in social context, and in health and disease. This critical mass of expertise is well-situated to provide early-career mentorship and collaborative opportunities for scholars asking foundational questions. Department faculty have access to the leading supercomputer center in the country (Texas Advanced Computing Center, TACC), the Children’s Research Center with facilities adjacent to the Priscilla Pond Flawn Child and Family Laboratory, a Siemens Prisma 3T scanner, and rich collaborative opportunities with colleagues in Human Development and Family Sciences, School of Computing, Neuroscience, Sociology, and Dell Medical School, among other units. The Department recently launched a new college-wide major in Behavioral and Social Data Science (BSDS), the first undergraduate degree program in the country devoted to producing data-science-ready graduates with a solid understanding of human psychology. Duties will include developing an independent sponsored research program, collaborating with other faculty from a wide range of backgrounds, mentoring and teaching graduate and undergraduate students, and serving the department, university, and profession. Qualifications Applicants must have completed all the requirements for the PhD by the time of appointment. Preferred qualifications include a record of research excellence demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications, a clearly defined research agenda, and demonstrated commitment to teaching and mentoring excellence. Application Instructions Interested candidates should upload a letter of interest (1 page), CV, research program statement (3 pages), teaching philosophy statement (1 page), up to 3 representative publications, and the names and contact information for 3-4 individuals who are willing to provide letters of recommendation. Additional materials may be requested after the initial review of applications. Review of applications will begin October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. For questions about the position, please contact Professor James Curley, Search Committee Chair curley@utexas.edu . For administrative questions about the application, please contact Tessa Green, tessa.green@austin.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Psychology Posted Sep 21, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191317": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Neuroscience (2027) Apply now Description The Department of Neuroscience at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor, who is working in the area of computational neuroscience and motivated to become an influential researcher, educator, and contributor to the scholarly community. The Department is seeking candidates involved in computational/theoretical approaches that bridge the gap between neuroscience and artificial intelligence, machine learning, and other areas of computational sciences.  We are seeking a new colleague whose work will synergize with our faculty engaged in cellular/molecular, cognitive/behavioral, and systems neuroscience. Other areas of interest include, but are not limited to, brain-computer interfaces, computational psychiatry, and other links to precision medicine and neuroscience research. This position entails highly competitive salary and start-up funds commensurate with level of appointment. Successful candidates will be expected to develop and maintain an active research program within a collegial, collaborative, and vibrant academic environment. The position will include full access to the Texas Advanced Computing Center supercomputers, and the option to join various multi-disciplinary neuroscience and advanced computing centers on campus, including the Center for Theoretical and Computational Neuroscience , the Center for Perceptual Systems , the Machine Learning Laboratory , the Center for Generative Artificial Intelligence , the Center for Learning and Memory , the Oden Institute for Computational Engineering and Science , and the Center for Computational Medicine . The University of Texas at Austin has strong and interactive research programs in Neuroscience, Psychology, Computer and Computational Science, Statistics, Mathematics, Engineering, Physics, Chemistry, and Molecular Biology, with an established culture of cross disciplinary collaboration. Austin is widely recognized as one of America’s most beautiful and livable cities, and the University recognizes the need to balance work and life. For information about the University’s life and work policies, including family friendly policies, visit: https://ombuds.utexas.edu/faculty/human-resources-worklife-balance . Qualifications The position requires a Ph.D. or equivalent degree with training in computer science, neuroscience, or a related field. Application Instructions Applications should be routed via the Department of Neuroscience within the College of Natural Sciences and all positions are subject to the availability of funding. Please apply via Interfolio ( apply.interfolio.com/193043 ) and upload: cover letter curriculum vitae one-page document describing current and future research programs statement describing candidate’s contributions to mentorship of students and other trainees three recommendation letters Applications received by November 22, 2026, will receive full consideration. Interdisciplinary candidates are encouraged to send applications to all relevant departments for better university-wide coordination. Candidates working at the intersection of Computer Science and Neuroscience are particularly encouraged to send applications to both departments to facilitate an explicit joint search. Please apply to the Computer Science position via Interfolio ( apply.interfolio.com/194129 ). Inquiries can be directed to Dr. Michael Mauk (mike@mail.clm.utexas.edu), Department of Neuroscience. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Neuroscience Posted Sep 21, 2026 Apply by Nov 22, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192380": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Jain Studies",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Assistant or Associate Professor of Jain Studies Apply now Description The Department of Asian Studies at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for the position of tenure-track Assistant or tenured Associate Professor of Jain Studies to begin Fall 2027. The position is open to candidates working in any discipline and time period within the humanities or social sciences. We seek candidates with excellent communication skills and broad theoretical training whose work will either strengthen or complement existing faculty interests in our department. Qualified candidates may be considered for research and program support through an endowment focused upon Jain Studies. The new faculty member will also have the opportunity to contribute to the South Asia Institute, as well as other departments and interdisciplinary programs in the College of Liberal Arts. Duties will include research and publication, undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentoring, and administrative service to the department, college, and university. Qualifications For hire as Assistant Professor, a pplicants must have completed all the requirements for the Ph.D. by time of appointment, or they must expect to obtain the Ph.D. within a year of joining the faculty as Instructor. For hire as Associate Professor, a Ph.D. degree is required. A research and teaching trajectory in the academic study of Jainism is required, including advanced competency in one or more Indic languages relevant for Jain Studies.  Preferred qualifications include demonstrated commitment to undergraduate and graduate teaching through innovative course topics and pedagogy. Application Instructions Applicants are invited to upload a letter of interest, CV, writing sample (article or chapter length), and a sample syllabus for an undergraduate course. Additional materials, including three letters of recommendation, may be requested after the initial review of applications. The committee will begin reviewing applications on October 30, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position should be directed to the chair of the search committee, Prof. Don Davis, drdj@austin.utexas.edu . Questions about the application process should be sent to Faith Hanley, faith.hanley@austin.utexas.edu . Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position funding is subject to budget availability. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Asian Studies Posted Sep 18, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192377": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Nursing Clinical Faculty, Professional Track (FY2026-27) Apply now Description The School of Nursing at The University of Texas at Austin is seeking applicants for professional-track positions including Clinical Instructor, Clinical Assistant Professor, and Clinical Associate Professor. Qualifications Candidates should hold a Master's in Nursing and have at least one year of clinical experience in their specialty area. Earned doctorate and teaching experience preferred. Prior to any appointment, candidates will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation. Successful candidates are expected to teach; be involved in service to the university, the community, and the profession; provide official college transcripts; and provide evidence of immunizations, current RN licensure, certifications as applicable, CPR, and PPD. Licensure, Certification, CPR, and PPD must be kept current while teaching. Application Instructions Applicants should upload a letter of interest and curriculum vitae via Interfolio. Materials should be addressed to: UTSON Hiring Committee The University of Texas at Austin School of Nursing 1710 Red River St. Austin, TX 78712 Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Nursing Posted Sep 18, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192318": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Professional Track Faculty Positions in Statistics and Data Science (2026-2027) Apply now Description The Department of Statistics and Data Science (SDS) at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for professional-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Instruction. SDS is the newest and fastest growing department in the College of Natural Sciences and is dedicated to delivering high-quality, innovative education in statistics and data science across the university. Our programs include an undergraduate major and minor in Statistics and Data Science, an M.S. and Ph.D. in Statistics, an undergraduate certificate in Applied Statistical Modeling, and an online M.S. in Data Science offered in collaboration with the Department of Computer Science. SDS is internationally recognized for excellence in statistical methodology and theory, applied statistics, data science, biostatistics, and machine learning. The department currently has 25 tenured/tenure-track and 10 professional-track faculty members.   While professional-track faculty are not eligible for tenure, we hire with the intent that all new faculty will build lasting careers as part of our community. The department is committed to investing in the professional growth of all of our faculty. Austin, the capital of Texas, is a center for high-technology industry, including companies such as 3M, Amazon, AMD, Apple, Applied Materials, AT&T, Dell, Google, IBM, National Instruments, and Samsung. Much of Austin’s lifestyle is driven by outdoor activities, media, and music. More information about the department is here . Qualifications Candidates must hold a doctoral degree in statistics, computer science, biostatistics, data science, or a related discipline by August 2027. We especially welcome applicants with teaching experience or professional practice as a statistician, biostatistician, or data scientist; these experiences are valued but not required. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a cover letter, a CV, and a teaching statement.  As part of the application process, candidates will be asked to provide the names and email addresses of two individuals who are willing to provide letters of support via Interfolio. A link to a professional website may be provided on the candidate’s CV. Applications for the Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Instruction positions will be reviewed on an ongoing basis, but priority will be given to applications received on or before February 5, 2027. The department is committed to respecting the confidentiality of the search process. A background check will be conducted on applicants selected for the positions. Questions about the search process should be directed to Professor Jay Bartroff, SDS Professional-Track Faculty Search Committee Chair and SDS Associate Chair.  Please send inquiries to statjobs@austin.utexas.edu instead of emailing the search committee chair directly. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Statistics and Data Science Posted Sep 18, 2026 Apply by Feb 5, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192275": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Dell Medical School Pediatric Neurologist Apply now Description About Dell Medical School As part of The University of Texas at Austin, one of the nation’s leading research universities, the Dell Medical School pursues innovation in the redesign of healthcare delivery, excellence in healthcare research, and programs in interdisciplinary and inter-professional education. It is the fifth medical school in the UT System and the first medical school established in nearly five decades by a member of the Association of American Universities, an organization of leading public and private research universities. The University of Texas Medical Center, anchored by Dell Medical School, is defining the future of health. Announced in August 2023 with planning and implementation underway, The University of Texas Medical Center will cement Austin as a premier destination for health care alongside other major Texas cities. With two new, state-of-the-art hospitals – A UT specialty hospital and a cancer center being built by MD Anderson – plus the academic and research expertise of UT, The University of Texas Medical Center will provide the integrated, world-class care that is a hallmark of academic medicine. The Dell Medical School is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to excellence within the learning health system. About The Program The Department of Neurology at The University of Texas Dell Medical School (DMS), in conjunction with UT Health Austin Pediatric Neurosciences at Dell Children’s, is recruiting a general pediatric neurologist to join our growing Pediatric Neurology faculty. The UT Health Austin Pediatric Neurosciences at Dell Children’s is a multidisciplinary program collaboration between Dell Medical School and Dell Children’s Medical Center that encompasses pediatric neurology, pediatric rehabilitation medicine, pediatric neurosurgery, pediatric neuropsychology, and pediatric neuro-ophthalmology. We are excited to expand our pediatric neurology faculty which is currently comprised of 17 pediatric neurologists and 10 nurse practitioners along with extensive allied professionals and staff including nursing, medical assistants, technicians, research associates, quality improvement experts, and well-being leaders. Since the neuroscience program’s inception in 2019, the pediatric neurology faculty has enjoyed rapid growth with subspecialty expertise in epilepsy, neuromuscular disorders, stroke, headache, neurogenetics, neurodevelopment, neonatal critical care, autonomics, and neuroimmunology. The hospital supports our consultation neurology services, and outpatient space is available in the adjoining 40,000 square foot neurosciences clinic within the Dell Children’s Specialty Pavilion. The pediatric neurosciences program currently hosts a child neurology residency along with fellowships in pediatric epilepsy, pediatric headache, and pediatric neuropsychology. Adult neurophysiology residents train in the pediatric program, and we plan to add fellowships in pediatric neuromuscular disease and pediatric rehabilitation medicine. Dell Children’s Medical Center is a 300-bed freestanding facility that features a level 1 pediatric trauma center, a level 4 neonatal intensive care unit, a level 4 epilepsy center, a CARF-accredited inpatient rehabilitation unit, and Magnet recognition for nursing excellence. About The Pediatric Neurologist Position The Department of Neurology at The University of Texas Dell Medical School (DMS), in conjunction with the UT Health Austin Pediatric Neurosciences at Dell Children’s, is recruiting a general pediatric neurologist to join the Dell Children’s Pediatric Neurology faculty. We seek an individual with excellent clinical skills, a passion for education, and the potential for scholarly contributions. Live in Austin a Vibrant, Lively, University City Known for its excellent schools, lively music scene, superb cuisine, and international festivals such as South by Southwest. The city typically ranks among the country’s best places to live. Nearby lakes and rivers as well as the beautiful adjacent Texas Hill Country provide ample opportunities for outdoor activities. Why Join Our Team We offer an attractive compensation and benefits package that is well rounded and competitive, making it desirable for individuals with excellent clinical skills, a passion for education, and potential for scholarly contributions to join a dynamic department in an innovative and growing medical school. Qualifications Candidates must hold an MD or DO degree and be ABPN certified or eligible in child neurology. Candidates must be eligible for a faculty appointment at Dell Medical School - The University of Texas at Austin. Hiring is contingent upon obtaining a Texas medical license and the appropriate hospital privileges. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest, a curriculum vitae, and any other appropriate material via Interfolio. The review of applications will be ongoing, and applications will be considered until the position is filled. The Department will contact applicants of interest for the next stage of the process. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Dell Medical School Department Department of Neurology Posted Sep 17, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192188": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Nursing Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track Apply now Description The University of Texas at Austin is seeking well-qualified, energetic, intellectually curious scholars to join our highly successful faculty in conducting cutting-edge research and teaching engaged students at one of the leading nursing schools in the nation. We are particularly interested in candidates with experience developing, implementing, or evaluating innovative care-delivery technologies and models that improve patient outcomes, expand access to care, and advance nursing practice. The University of Texas at Austin offers an exceptional environment for interdisciplinary research and collaboration. Faculty have opportunities to engage with scholars, clinicians, and research teams across the University, including Dell Medical School, and its numerous research centers and institutes. The University supports more than 3,500 externally sponsored projects, 150 dedicated research units and centers, and annual research funding exceeding $500 million. The School of Nursing offers BSN, MSN, PhD, and DNP degrees in nursing. Additionally, the School has an alternate entry MSN for persons with baccalaureate degrees outside of nursing, and accelerated BSN to PhD and BSN to DNP options. The School is committed to advancing nursing science through innovative research, education, clinical practice, and service. The University of Texas at Austin is located in the capitol of Texas. Austin is regarded as an open-minded, friendly, and innovative city. The city has been consistently rated a national creative center that draws talented people from across the world to its high quality of life, abundant resources, lively entertainment, and active lifestyle. As an added benefit, the children of professional nurse faculty who are under the age of 25 years are eligible for a full tuition waiver at UT Austin. Qualifications Successful Candidates are Expected To: Develop and/or maintain a research program in nursing/health science. Teach and advise undergraduate and/or graduate students. Provide service to the school of nursing, the university, the community and the profession. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate Evidence of post-doctoral training Strong track record of data-based publications Evidence of, or strong potential for, an extramurally funded program of research in areas of care innovation. Eligibility for RN licensure in the State of Texas Application Instructions Applicants should upload the following materials through Interfolio: Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Materials should be addressed to: Kavita Radhakrishnan, PhD, MSEE, RN, FAAN Dean Laura Lee Blanton Chair in Nursing Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Nursing Posted Sep 17, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192085": {
      "title": "Open Rank - Associate or Assistant Professor Fall 2027",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Open Rank - Associate or Assistant Professor Fall 2027 Apply now Description The Department of Mathematics at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for two positions at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor or of Associate Professor with tenure, with the intent of appointing the two successful candidates as a Fellow of the Sid W. Richardson Foundation Regents Chair in Mathematics #2 and #3, respectively. Highly qualified senior candidates at the full professor level are welcome to apply. Each position is a nine-month appointment with a start date of August 16, 2027. The department is interested in candidates who will contribute to excellence in mentoring through teaching, research, and service. The minimum annual 9-month salary is $120,000 with Health and Retirement Benefits. The salary can be negotiated based on experience. The applications review process will start on October 19, 2026 . Applications will be reviewed on a continuous basis until both positions are filled. Qualifications Qualified candidates must have a Ph.D. degree and comparable experience with a strong research focus in mathematics and have publications in high-quality outlets, possess a research agenda that is well-defined and shows promise for strong external funding potential. In addition, the candidates should have positive evidence of excellence in mathematics education. Preference will be given to applicants whose research interests can integrate and broaden the current research areas represented in the department. Helpful Links: Faculty Careers College of Natural Science - Math Application Instructions Application Materials Required : Interested applicants should apply and submit the following items online at UT Austin Faculty Careers : https://apply.interfolio.com/193738 Cover Letter that includes research area, qualifications, and contact information. Curriculum Vitae Teaching statement Research statement Publication List Four signed letters of recommendation (to be submitted online by the reference writers at this site with one letter speaking to the applicant’s teaching credentials) Background Checks: A criminal history background check will be required for finalist(s) under consideration for this position. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Mathematics Posted Sep 16, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192014": {
      "title": "Faculty Position (Tenured)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Department of Chemistry Faculty Position (Tenured) Apply now Description The Department of Chemistry at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for tenured faculty positions at the rank of full professor in all areas of chemistry, subject to available funding. We are particularly interested in candidates with exceptional and internationally recognized experimental research programs, who will help to further elevate our research and higher educational profile through their leadership, mentorship, teaching and service. It is anticipated that a successful candidate for this position will be awarded a personal chair to support their future research program at UT Austin. The Department of Chemistry's mission is to provide all our students with an innovative and forward-looking scientific education grounded in the fundamentals of chemistry. The department and University have state-of-the-art core facilities and we are ranked 16th best graduate program in the United States by US News and World Report (2023 ed.). We have a strong commitment to building and maintaining a supportive environment where all people can thrive. Learn more about the Department of Chemistry and key core facilities, including Mass Spectrometry , Nuclear Magnetic Resonance , and the X-Ray Facility . Qualifications PhD in chemistry or closely related field. Application Instructions Complete applications should contain a cover letter indicating area of expertise, a curriculum vitae, a description of research plans, a teaching statement, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation will be requested directly from the letter writers through Interfolio. The University of Texas at Austin is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Department of Chemistry Posted Sep 16, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192013": {
      "title": "Faculty Position (Tenured; Director, Bard Center for Electrochemistry)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Department of Chemistry Faculty Position (Tenured; Director, Bard Center for Electrochemistry) Apply now Description The Department of Chemistry at the University of Texas at Austin is seeking to hire a tenured faculty member in experimental electrochemistry, who would also assume the role of Director of the Bard Center for Electrochemistry (Bard CEC), subject to available funding.  We are particularly interested in candidates who are internationally recognized academic leaders in electrochemistry research (broadly defined).  This position will provide a unique opportunity to lead the future direction and growth of the acclaimed Bard CEC, and to expand its impact across the electrochemical sciences at UT Austin and beyond. This position is associated with a personal chair as part of the Center Directorship.  A successful candidate will also be a dynamic department colleague, demonstrated through a sustained record of excellence in teaching, research, mentoring, and service. The Bard Center for Electrochemistry was founded by Professor Allen J. Bard in 2006 with support from the Welch Foundation, to capitalize on a half century of excellence in electrochemistry at the University of Texas at Austin.  The CEC fosters collaborative research programs in the electrochemical sciences. Its broad mission is to advance research and solve problems, fundamental or applied, related to ion and electorn transfer at interfaces.  The Bard CEC connects fundamental electrochemistry with materials science and engineering, with applications in diverse fields such as, energy, sustainability, health, and emerging technologies. It is comprised of a multi-disciplinary group of more than 250 faculty, staff, and student researchers spanning the chemistry, materials, and engineering aspects of electrochemical science.  See: electrochemistry.utexas.edu for more details. The Department of Chemistry's mission is to provide all our students with an innovative and forward-looking scientific education grounded in the fundamentals of chemistry. The department and University have state-of-the-art core facilities and we are ranked 16th best graduate program in the United States by US News and World Report (2023 ed.).  We have a strong commitment to building and maintaining a supportive environment where all people can thrive. Qualifications PhD in chemistry or closely related field. Application Instructions Complete applications should contain a cover letter indicating area of expertise, a curriculum vitae, a description of research plans, a teaching statement, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation will be requested directly from the letter writers through Interfolio. The University of Texas at Austin is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Department of Chemistry Posted Sep 16, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191973": {
      "title": "Open-Rank Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Positions",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Open-Rank Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Positions Apply now Description Description The newly formed School of Computing is entering a period of significant growth. As one of the three founding departments of the School of Computing, and as part of this growth process, the Department of Information (iSchool) at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for tenured and tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, to begin Fall 2027. Candidates will be expected to conduct innovative and impactful research and to teach at both graduate and undergraduate levels. Our tenure-track and tenured faculty normally have a 2/1 teaching load, with opportunities for course releases and buyouts. Salaries are competitive. This is an open-rank, open-area search. We welcome applications from exceptional candidates in any area. W e encourage applicants from a broad range of fields to apply. Examples of areas include but are not limited to: • Archives and Digital Curation • AI and Health • Human-Centered AI • Human-Centered Data Science • Human-Computer Interaction and Design • Information Behavior • Information Security and Privacy We are particularly interested in information scholars whose expertise spans more than one of these areas, focuses on their intersections, or connects these areas to other areas of the information field and beyond. The Department holds the Governor Bill Daniel Professorship in Archival Enterprise, which is one of multiple endowments available to support appointments made through this search. The iSchool is one of three founding departments of UT's new School of Computing, alongside Computer Science and Statistics and Data Science. We welcome candidates whose work creates opportunities for collaboration with colleagues in those departments or elsewhere at The University of Texas at Austin, including through joint appointments. As an interdisciplinary academic unit, our faculty are drawn from a wide range of disciplines and collaborate broadly with other units across campus. We welcome applications from various disciplines including, but not limited to: information science, information studies, computer science, data science, archival studies, library and information science, human-centered computing, science and technology studies, communication, psychology, design, computational linguistics, and electrical and computer engineering. We seek strong scholars with compelling research agendas, regardless of disciplinary background. Applicants, especially those unfamiliar with iSchools, are welcome to contact us prior to submitting an application to discuss how their research connects to our department at facultysearch@ischool.utexas.edu. See resources from the international iSchools organization for additional background. Research Resources and Context Faculty hired through this search will find substantial resources and collaborators at UT Austin, within the School of Computing, and within the Department. UT Austin has a world-class reputation for AI research and education. The university's online MS in Artificial Intelligence is the largest AI program at any tier-one university, complementing existing online master's programs in computer science and data science. The Center for Generative AI hosts the largest academic GPU cluster in the world and includes one of our faculty members on its team. The Texas Advanced Computing Center provides world-class AI compute resources and received a $457M award from the National Science Foundation to create a new leadership-class computing facility. UT also leads the $20M NSF AI Institute for Foundations of Machine Learning, the $20M NSF-Simons AI Institute for Cosmic Origins, and the $30M NSF Center for Human and Robot Co-Adaptation. All three involve our faculty. Health is among the most active areas of AI work at UT Austin. Dell Medical School launched a new Department of Quantitative and Systems Health Sciences in August 2026, integrating artificial intelligence, data science, and systems-level approaches to advance discovery and health care innovation. Dell Med, the School of Nursing, the School of Social Work, and the College of Pharmacy are all on the same campus, and our faculty collaborate with colleagues across them on consumer health informatics, biomedical data, and the social dimensions of health. Strengthening these connections is a priority for the School of Computing, including through joint appointments. Responsible AI is central to UT Austin's vision for these technologies. Good Systems, the university's campus-wide responsible AI initiative, includes more than 120 researchers across 30 academic units and integrates technology, policy, law, and ethics in the pursuit of societal good. Our faculty have played a prominent role in Good Systems, serving on its leadership team and leading its core research projects. Two of our faculty secured a five-year, $3M NSF Research Traineeship award addressing the integration of responsible and ethical AI throughout the development, design, and deployment of AI technologies, which led to a new cross-campus Graduate Portfolio Program in Ethical AI. Additional work on responsible AI in the Department includes research on AI policy, design, auditing, and data collection and stewardship, including the ethical sourcing of AI training data. The Department's Information eXperience Lab supports research on how people seek, evaluate, and use information, with eye-tracking, physiological measures, and usability facilities. The lab is a shared resource for work in information behavior, human-computer interaction, and human-centered data science, and it is available to faculty across the university. Austin is an unusually rich setting for archival research and education, featuring the Texas State Library and Archives Commission and the Lyndon B. Johnson Presidential Library. UT Austin is home to the Harry Ransom Center, the Briscoe Center for American History, and the LLILAS Benson Latin American Collections. Within the Department, our Conservation Lab supports hands-on teaching and research in the care and treatment of collections. About the Department of Information Founded in 1948, we are a founding member of the iSchools Caucus and a founding department of the School of Computing. We work at the intersection of people, information, and technology. We study how people organize, preserve, and trust information; how technology meets the people who use it; and how computing and society shape one another. We offer BA and BS degrees in Informatics, an ALA-accredited Master of Science in Information Studies, a Master of Science in Information Security and Privacy offered jointly with the UT Center for Identity, and a PhD in Information Studies. About the School of Computing Computing is bigger than any one discipline, and the biggest breakthroughs happen where technology, data, and people come together. The School of Computing brings together world-class expertise, exceptional computing power, pioneering research, and deep industry connections to address problems no single field can solve alone. Its three founding departments are Information, Computer Science, and Statistics and Data Science. About The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin has an internationally distinguished faculty, making it one of the world's leading research universities. The university embraces interdisciplinary research and is home to more than 200 dedicated research units, centers, and research grand challenges. About Austin Austin is the capital of Texas and a major technology center, home to 3M, Amazon, AMD, Apple, Applied Materials, Dell, Google, IBM, Meta, and Samsung, along with a large startup community. It is also home to substantial cultural heritage institutions, a vibrant music, film, and art scene, and the Austin City Limits and South by Southwest festivals. The city sits in the Hill Country of central Texas, with a river running through its center, and appears regularly in lists of top places to live and work. Qualifications Applicants must hold a doctoral degree in a field relevant to their area of research and teaching, or convincingly demonstrate that they will complete the degree before starting (for example, by documenting a scheduled defense). Candidates for appointment at the rank of Associate or Full Professor with tenure must have a substantial record of research, teaching, and service commensurate with that rank at a leading research university. We seek candidates with outstanding records of research, teaching, mentoring, service, and leadership, with a commitment to shaping the future of the Department and the information field more broadly. Applications should make a compelling case for the intellectual contribution and merit of the candidate's research agenda. Candidates are encouraged to identify the research communities to which they belong and to which their research trajectory will contribute. Application Instructions The search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 15, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted and considered until the positions are filled. Required materials: • A current CV. • A cover letter (1–2 pages suggested). • A research statement (2–4 pages suggested) highlighting significance, impact, and future plans. • Up to three samples of your written work, such as peer-reviewed articles to which you contributed significantly. • A teaching statement (1–2 pages suggested) highlighting your teaching experience and philosophy as well as existing and new courses you would like to teach. • Names and contact details of three references who have agreed to provide a reference letter. We will contact references later in the process, if warranted. Candidates are encouraged to indicate in their cover letters any opportunities for collaboration or joint or courtesy appointments with other units within or beyond the School of Computing. Please direct all questions regarding this search to facultysearch@ischool.utexas.edu. All faculty positions are based in Austin, Texas. Successful candidates residing outside the Austin area will be expected to relocate. Applications can be submitted via Interfolio's Faculty Search solution. If you do not have a Dossier account with Interfolio, you will be prompted to create a free account prior to applying. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department Information Posted Sep 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191947": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Instruction & Program Manager, Communication for Engineering Students",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Moody College of Communication Assistant Professor of Instruction & Program Manager, Communication for Engineering Students Apply now Description The Moody College of Communication is seeking a full-time, professional-track faculty member to coordinate, manage, and teach a large, multi-section hybrid course on business and professional communication in collaboration with the Cockrell School of Engineering. No experience in the field of engineering is required. Moody College includes programs in Advertising and Public Relations, Communication Studies, Journalism and Media, Radio-Television-Film, and Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences. The faculty appointment may be in any of the College’s schools or departments, depending on the candidate’s expertise and experience. The ideal candidate for this role will be able to communicate effectively and maintain positive working relationships with a range of campus partners in multiple disciplines. They will demonstrate professionalism and responsiveness in both electronic and interpersonal interactions. The ideal candidate is organized, proactive, and demonstrates a high level of solution-oriented thinking, maturity, and integrity. Moody College of Communication: http://moody.utexas.edu/ Position Duties and Responsibilities Teaching duties and course assignments will depend on the interests and capabilities of the individual and departmental instructional needs. Job responsibilities will include coordinating assigned sections of the engineering communication course each long semester and each summer, along with sections abroad during May term, while executing the duties associated with course management for all sections. These duties may include: Developing and revising curriculum as necessary. Supervising and training current instructors and teaching assistants on course materials such as lectures, activities, and assignments. Identifying and onboarding new instructors and teaching assistants as necessary. Overseeing day-to-day operations of the course, including course syllabi and course web pages. Providing data-driven updates to leadership and other campus stakeholders. Providing support for periodic accreditation reviews. Acting as the liaison between the Moody College, Cockrell School, and the technical support team. Qualifications Strong candidates for this role will have the following: A doctorate in Communication Studies, Journalism, or a related field. Demonstrated teaching excellence in the field of communication at the college level. Experience managing a multi-section course with a large teaching team. Experience teaching or managing a hybrid course. Experience in training or learning development (e.g., supervising teaching assistants). Experience working in a cross-functional or interdisciplinary environment. Experience with instructional design, especially in hybrid environments. Experience with technical, science, or other STEM-related communication. An exceptional command of written and spoken Standard American English, contributing to the clear and effective communication of ideas. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, evidence of teaching effectiveness, and the names and contact information for three references. Letters of recommendation will be requested of finalists. Screening of applicants will begin from the date the position is posted and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must be made via Interfolio. If you do not have a Dossier account with Interfolio, you will be prompted to create one prior to applying for the position. If you have questions about using Interfolio, please email help@interfolio.com or call (877) 997-8807. Questions can be directed to the chair of the search committee, Anneke Chy, at Anneke.chy@austin.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Moody College of Communication Posted Sep 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191913": {
      "title": "Part-Time Clinical Professor, Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Law Part-Time Clinical Professor, Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic Apply now Description The University of Texas School of Law seeks to hire a part-time clinical professor to teach in its Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic (ECDC). The successful candidate may choose either a half-time or three-quarter-time appointment and will work alongside the clinic’s existing faculty, Professor Frances Leos Martinez, Clinic Director, and Professor Heather Way. The clinical professor will supervise students in their representation of clients and will meet regularly with students to mentor and advise them. The clinical professor will be responsible for ensuring that the clinic’s clients receive competent and professional representation. The professor will also collaborate with the clinic faculty in all aspects of the clinic’s work, including teaching portions of the clinic’s weekly seminar, selecting cases and projects, and participating in activities of the broader clinical program. Clinical faculty positions at Texas Law are nine-month appointments. This position is a part-time, non-tenure-track clinical faculty appointment, with all accompanying health and other benefits. Depending on the needs of the clinic’s operations, cases and projects, an additional part-time summer appointment may be available. The clinical professor will join a close community of over 25 faculty members who teach in Texas Law’s clinical program. The salary range for this half-time position is $75,000 - $86,000 for nine months, depending on experience. The salary for a three-quarter-time appointment will be adjusted proportionally. Any summer appointment would be compensated separately on a pro rata basis. About the Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic The ECDC provides pro bono transactional legal representation to small businesses, social enterprises, creatives, nonprofit organizations, collectives, cooperatives, and community groups in Texas. The clinic’s clients are community enterprises that strengthen their communities by creating jobs, producing and preserving affordable housing, expanding asset-building opportunities for people with low incomes, and providing innovative and valuable goods and services. The ECDC has a dual mission: educating law students and serving low-income communities through the practice of transactional law. More information about the ECDC is available at: https://law.utexas.edu/clinics/ecdc/ Qualifications Membership in good standing with the State Bar of Texas or eligibility for admission to the Texas Bar without examination. At least six years of transactional law experience in one or more relevant practice areas, such as corporate law, nonprofit or tax law, community economic development, microenterprise, intellectual property, or real estate. Experience supervising and mentoring law students or junior attorneys. Familiarity with clinical teaching methods. Preferred Qualifications Prior teaching experience (preferred). Experience working with community groups. Application Instructions To apply, submit a letter of interest, a resume or curriculum vitae, a writing sample, and the name and contact information of three references. Review of applications will begin in October 20, 2026, and will continue on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The preferred start date for the position is July or August 2027, although the successful candidate may have the option to begin as early as January 2027. Questions about the position should be directed to Professor Frances Leos Martinez, Director of the Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic, at fmartinez@law.utexas.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Law Posted Sep 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191722": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Fall 2027 - Multiple Positions in Mechanical Engineering - UT Austin Apply now Description The Walker Department of Mechanical Engineering at The University of Texas at Austin seeks applicants for multiple positions. Candidates with expertise in the following research areas are of the most interest to the Department. (Candidates should view these areas of emphasis in the broadest sense in determining whether they match their research skills and interests.) NUCLEAR ENGINEERING, with an emphasis on nuclear materials or radiochemistry. Candidates should be able to demonstrate an ability to teach both nuclear engineering and materials science or mechanical engineering courses at the undergraduate level. ROBOTICS & PHYSICAL AI, with an emphasis on embodied autonomous and semi-autonomous systems that perceive, plan, and act under real-world physical constraints, including in close collaboration with humans, spanning: (a) novel actuator and sensor design, including soft and smart materials and metamaterials, and their integration into robotic systems; (b) physics-inspired machine learning for perception, planning, and control, together with the experimental data collection and simulation tools needed to support it, with a balanced approach to theory and experimental work; (c) applications in assistive and rehabilitation robotics that restore, augment, or support human motor, sensory, or cognitive function, including emerging brain-robot interfaces, and in intelligent automation for complex, unstructured industrial, service, or medical environments. ADVANCED MANUFACTURING , with an emphasis on advancing additive manufacturing as a production technology for aerospace, defense, and other critical applications. Research may span the technologies needed to make additive manufacturing more intelligent, predictable, and certifiable, including machine-learning-enabled process control, in situ sensing and monitoring, data-driven and physics-based modeling, digital twins, materials-by-design, and integrated process-structure-property relationships. Of particular interest are candidates whose work can reduce the time and cost associated with process and part qualification by moving quality assurance from post-build inspection to predictive, in-process understanding and control. The successful candidate will strengthen UT Austin's leadership in production-focused additive manufacturing and advance the field toward reliable, autonomous processes. Candidates at the rank of Assistant Professor are sought for the Nuclear and Robotics searches. In the Advanced Manufacturing search applications are welcomed at both the Assistant Professor and Tenured Associate/Full Professor ranks. Qualifications Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. degree in Engineering or a related field prior to their start date. Successful candidates will be expected to create undergraduate and graduate learning environments that address the needs of students from a variety of backgrounds, with differing learning approaches and abilities, develop an externally sponsored research program, mentor graduate students, collaborate with other faculty, and be involved in service to the university and the profession. The Walker Department of Mechanical Engineering is committed to supporting the success of all members of our community: students, staff, and faculty. A successful candidate can expect to benefit from and contribute to those efforts. More information about the Walker Department of Mechanical Engineering, including a summary of the Department’s Strategic Plan , may be found at www.me.utexas.edu. Application Instructions Application Interested applicants should submit the following materials via Interfolio: (1) a cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) research statement, (4) teaching and mentoring statement, and (5) a list of references. Please see me.utexas.edu/people/faculty-jobs for additional information and guidance. Successful candidates will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form, a background check, and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the U.S.A. Deadlines For full consideration, applications should be received by November 8, 2026. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Mechanical Engineering Posted Sep 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191658": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions McCombs School of Business 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty - Marketing Apply now Description The Department of Marketing periodically hires non-tenure track faculty to instruct undergraduate and graduate courses in marketing, including hiring in the Lecturer, Professor of Instruction, and Clinical series titles. Needed courses may range from introductory to advanced areas of specialization in marketing theory and practice related to marketing strategy, analytics, and consumer insights. Areas of special interest include product marketing, digital marketing, marketing analytics methods, professional sales, branding, global marketing, marketing communications, consumer behavior, and related topics. Specialized skills in new marketing methods and tools are also valued. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent on funding. Qualifications A master's degree, high level corporate marketing expertise, specialized marketing skills, and evidence of teaching success are generally preferred, but we will consider applicants with a variety of strengths. Candidates should have potential for excellence in teaching and excellent communication skills. Application Instructions Submit cover letter that includes information about courses or topics you are able to offer, and information about teaching and /or teaching related experience, a CV, and a minimum of three letters of recommendation via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/173119 Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit McCombs School of Business Department Department of Marketing Posted Sep 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191310": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track/Tenured Faculty Positions in Statistics and Data Science (2026-2027)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Tenure-Track/Tenured Faculty Positions in Statistics and Data Science (2026-2027) Apply now Description The Department of Statistics and Data Science (SDS) at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for tenured or tenure-track faculty positions, at any rank, to begin in August 2027. We seek exceptional scholars whose work advances our ability to learn from data, whether by directly addressing important empirical questions in a chosen area of application, or by deepening the foundations of statistical inference and learning. We are particularly interested in four broad directions. First, we seek candidates in applied statistics, scientific machine learning, or AI for science: methodologists embedded deeply enough in another field that they help shape its scientific questions and build new data-analysis methods in response. The field may be any area of the natural, biomedical, computational, engineering, or social sciences. For such candidates, we regard publication in the leading venues of their chosen field as central evidence of impact, on equal footing with publication in statistics and machine-learning venues. No matter the area, the candidate's record should exhibit methodological advances that statisticians and machine-learning researchers would recognize as such, even if they first appeared in a domain journal. Second, we welcome research in causal inference, experimental design, and related areas concerned with learning from interventions and designing informative studies. This includes foundational and applied work on how interventions are identified and evaluated, how evidence generalizes across settings, and how experiments and other data-collection strategies can be designed to answer important questions. Third, we seek scholars working on statistical theory, including the foundations of machine learning and AI. We are interested in fundamental questions about inference, uncertainty, information, learning, robustness, computation, and decision-making. We value theoretical and computational work that provides new understanding of (or new broad capabilities for) statistics, machine learning, and AI, whether or not it is tied to an immediate application. Fourth, we welcome work that creates broadly useful new computational tools for data-analytic practice, such as statistical computing environments and languages, probabilistic programming systems, scalable inference software, and interactive or AI-assisted tools for data analysis. For such candidates, widespread adoption of their tools by researchers and practitioners is evidence of impact on equal footing with publication. SDS is one of three founding departments of UT's new School of Computing, alongside Computer Science and Information. We welcome candidates whose work creates opportunities for collaboration with colleagues in those departments or elsewhere at The University of Texas at Austin, including through joint appointments. Our department is internationally recognized for its research in statistical methodology and theory, machine learning, applied statistics, Bayesian inference, and biostatistics, and its faculty are committed to excellent teaching in statistics and data science for students from across the School, the University, and in SDS's own degree programs. The department currently has 26 tenured/tenure-track faculty, including 9 joint faculty with primary appointments in other departments. UT Austin is one of the most intellectually vibrant universities in the country, with abundant opportunities for interdisciplinary research within the College of Natural Sciences and School of Computing, and across the Dell Medical School, the Oden Institute for Computational Engineering and Sciences, the Population Research Center, the Machine Learning Laboratory, the Center for Generative AI, and many other research centers across the campus. A partnership with the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC) provides access to world-class computing resources.  The department and university are committed to supporting the professional development of all members of the faculty.  The teaching load for tenured/tenure-track faculty in SDS is two courses per year. Austin, the capital of Texas, is a center for high-technology industry, including companies such as 3M, Amazon, AMD, Apple, Applied Materials, AT&T, Dell, Google, IBM, National Instruments, and Samsung. Much of Austin’s lifestyle is driven by outdoor activities, media, and music. More information about the department is here . Qualifications Candidates should have a doctoral degree in statistics, biostatistics, computer science, machine learning, applied mathematics, or a closely related discipline by August 2027. Candidates for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position are evaluated based on their potential for developing an impactful research program and for becoming excellent in teaching, mentoring, and service. Candidates for a tenured Associate or Full Professor position are expected to exhibit a strong independent program of externally funded research along with established records of excellence in teaching, mentoring, and service. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a cover letter, a CV, and separate statements summarizing the applicant’s contributions and plans in the areas of research, teaching, and mentoring.  As part of the application submission process, candidates will be asked to provide the names and email addresses of three individuals who are willing to supply letters of support via Interfolio. For applicants currently in graduate school, at least two letters should be from faculty members at the candidate’s graduate institution. Candidates applying for a tenured position may decline to provide letters of support at the initial application stage.  A link to a professional website where published papers, preprints, and software packages are available may be provided on the candidate’s CV. Review of a pplications will begin on November 13, 2026 and will continue until the positions are filled. The department is committed to respecting the confidentiality of the search process. A background check will be conducted on applicants selected for the positions. Questions about the search process should be directed to statjobs@austin.utexas.edu , for the attention of Professors Roger Peng and Alessandro Rinaldo, SDS Faculty Search Committee Co-Chairs. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Statistics and Data Science Posted Sep 14, 2026 Apply by Nov 13, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191781": {
      "title": "Professional Track Faculty Position",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Professional Track Faculty Position - Chemical Engineering - 2026-27 Apply now Description The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering seeks to develop a pool of candidates for professional track faculty positions to teach undergraduate and graduate level courses. Positions are part-time and contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Positions may not be available every year. The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering’s undergraduate and graduate programs are both top-10 ranked programs in the nation according to U.S. News & World Report. The department leads pioneering research and education in vital areas such as energy and the environment, human health, materials and manufacturing. Our faculty includes 12 members of the National Academy of Engineering and two National Medal of Technology and Innovation recipients. Our students go on to transform industries, benefit society and improve quality of life. As part of the Cockrell School of Engineering, we’re committed to building a welcoming environment, free from all forms of hate, racism and injustice, where students, faculty and staff feel safe and supported. Every member of our school community — whoever you are, wherever you are from and whatever you believe — is valued. We are proud to be an Equal Opportunity Employer. Qualifications A Ph.D. in chemical engineering or a related field is required, but exceptions can be made for applicants with a Master's degree and pertinent expertise and work experience. Previous teaching and/or training experience is preferred. SALARY: Dependent on position and qualifications. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Interested applicants should submit a detailed resume including academic, training, and technical experience, a statement regarding their specific teaching interests and qualifications, and a list of three references. Further information about the McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering may be found at che.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Chemical Engineering Posted Sep 11, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191765": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Government - 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty Pool Apply now Description The Department of Government at The University of Texas at Austin seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/non-tenure track positions to teach undergraduate and graduate courses. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Appointments are made on a semester-by-semester basis, though sometimes there is departmental need for an annual appointment. Qualifications A Ph.D. or extensive professional/occupational experience relevant to political science is required. Previous teaching experience is preferred. Application Instructions Interested applicants are invited to submit a detailed curriculum vitae, three letters of recommendation, transcript, and teaching materials. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Government Posted Sep 11, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191743": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Assistant Professor, Fall 2027 - Aerospace Engineering Apply now Description The Department of Aerospace Engineering and Engineering Mechanics at The University of Texas at Austin  invites applicants for a full-time tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. The field of aerospace engineering is undergoing revolutionary transformation in 1) Space discovery, observations, and connectivity and in 2) Advanced air mobility solutions.  Applicants with expertise in all disciplines pertinent to these two application areas are encouraged to apply for the position.  We seek exceptional candidates who hold promise for leading these transformations. An emphasis will be placed on applicants who have a clear vision about how they will contribute to these emerging areas of aerospace engineering. The Department of Aerospace Engineering and Engineering Mechanics is committed to supporting the success of all members of our community: students, staff, and faculty. A successful candidate can expect to benefit from and contribute to these efforts. Qualifications Candidates must have a PhD in engineering or a related discipline. The successful candidate will be expected to create undergraduate and graduate learning environments that address the needs of students from a variety of backgrounds, with differing learning styles and abilities. Further, the successful applicant will be expected to develop an externally sponsored research program, mentor graduate students, collaborate with other faculty, and be involved in service to the university and profession. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit the following materials: (1) a cover letter, (2) current curriculum vitae, (3) research statement, (4) teaching and mentoring statement, and (5) three professional reference letters . Please see https://ae.utexas.edu/people/careers/ for additional information and guidance. Candidates should address their submission to the Aerospace Engineering Faculty Search Committee. Evaluation of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 , and will continue until an appropriate candidate is identified. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Please address questions to Prof. David Goldstein, the Faculty Search Committee Chair ( david@oden.utexas.edu ) Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Aerospace Engineering & Engineering Mechanics Posted Sep 11, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191518": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Assistant Professor - Biomechanics and Neurorehabilitation Apply now Description The Department of Kinesiology and Health Education at The University of Texas at Austin invites qualified applicants for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in the area of Biomechanics and Neurorehabilitation. We are seeking a highly productive scholar with expertise in biomechanical and neuromuscular mechanisms of human movement and their role in preventing the onset, progression, or recurrence of adverse health and behavioral outcomes. The Biomechanics and Neurorehabilitation area of our department combines the traditional fields of biomechanics; motor development, motor control, and learning; and cognitive and neuromuscular function. This program examines multidisciplinary aspects of how people move in a variety of settings, including clinical applications, elite performance, and typical changes related to development and aging. The ultimate goal of our work in this area is to generate research which can translate into the improvement of clinical and functional outcomes for people across different stages of life. Our ideal candidate will have a prevention-oriented research agenda that uses biomechanics and/or neuromuscular control to examine mechanisms related to adverse health and behavioral outcomes or develops interventions targeting these mechanisms and pathways to improve health and behavioral outcomes. Integral to program areas in Kinesiology and Health Education and throughout the college, the candidate will build interdisciplinary collaborations linking biomechanics and neuromuscular control with behavioral, clinical, community, or public health prevention frameworks. We expect the successful candidate to have a strong publication record and to demonstrate a record of, or potential for, securing external grants to support their research. In addition, the successful candidate will show potential to be an excellent mentor and will demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching coursework in their areas of expertise and within biomechanics, movement science, and other biomechanics and neurorehabilitation sub-disciplinary areas. We seek an individual who is committed to contributing as a faculty member, researcher, and through service to our academic community. Our department is currently ranked #6 by the National Academy of Kinesiology, and we are located within the College of Education, recently ranked #6 by U.S. News & World Report . Our department is currently home to more than 2,000 undergraduates and almost 300 master’s and doctoral students. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students’ diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and many other academic and research units on campus. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Qualifications REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in biomechanics, kinesiology, physiology, exercise physiology, or related scientific discipline earned by July 31, 2027 Record of, or potential for, research excellence as demonstrated by refereed publications, extramural funding, and evidence of professional service Experience or capacity to teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students Application Instructions Review of applications will begin October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, please provide all requested materials by October 15, 2026. Applicants should upload the following items to the Interfolio system: Cover Letter with contact information Full C.V. Research Statement that includes research accomplishments and future research direction Teaching and Mentoring Statement that describes specific methods used to ensure effective student mentoring and a classroom environment that is welcoming, engaging, and supportive of student learning and success. Up to three representative publications A list of three references with contact information. Letters are not required at the time of application. For additional information about the department, visit our website https://education.utexas.edu/departments/kinesiology-health-education/ For questions regarding the application process, please contact Isabella Miluzzo at isabella.miluzzo@austin.utexas.edu . Questions about the position can be directed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Hao Hsiao, at HHsiao@austin.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Kinesiology and Health Education Posted Sep 10, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191506": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Philosophy - 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty Pool Apply now Description The Department of Philosophy seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/nontenured positions to teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Appointments are usually made on a semester-by-semester basis, though sometimes there is departmental need for an annual appointment. Positions are renewable annually, contingent upon budgetary funding and performance. Qualifications A PhD in Philosophy and a record of teaching excellence are preferred. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit a current curriculum vita, recent syllabi, course evaluations, and 3 professional letters of reference. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Philosophy Posted Sep 9, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/190881": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Human Development",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Human Development Apply now Description The Department of Educational Psychology in the College of Education at the University of Texas-Austin is seeking a tenure-track Assistant Professor in the area of Human Development. The person hired for this position will conduct research examining how developmental processes shape learning and well-being across the lifespan, with particular attention to the cultural, social, and institutional contexts in which development occurs. We are especially interested in scholarship that advances understanding of how cultural values, practices, identities, families, communities, and institutions shape developmental processes. We envision a scholar whose work uses developmental science to address questions of central importance to education broadly defined. Relevant areas include but are not limited to how cognitive, social, emotional, or neurobiological development shapes educational experiences; how developmental processes vary across cultural and sociocultural contexts; how family, school, community, and broader institutional environments interact with individual development; how developmental transitions shape educational experiences and well-being ; or how educational environments can better support development across groups. We particularly welcome scholars who incorporate a developmental neuroscience perspective, while also encouraging applicants who use other rigorous and innovative theoretical, methodological, or interdisciplinary approaches. Across approaches, we seek a scholar whose research can contribute to developmental and educational research, practice, programs, or policy by deepening understanding of how people develop within the contexts in which education occurs. UT-Austin's Department of Educational Psychology has robust graduate programs and is currently ranked #6 among the Best Educational Psychology Programs rated by U.S. News & World Report . The department advances knowledge about the human experience at the intersection of education and psychology through research, teaching, and practice. It provides an interdisciplinary and contemporary approach to learning and development and seeks to enhance the quality of the human experience through rigorous educational and psychological research, and effective translation of findings to policy and practice. The successful candidate will have opportunities to build collaborations across the College of Education and the broader university. Qualifications REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in Human Development, Developmental Psychology, Educational Psychology, Developmental Science, or a closely related field earned by August 2027. Potential for scholarly leadership (i.e., refereed publications and extramural funding) in the area of human development, with research that examines development within cultural, contextual, and institutional settings and contributes to understanding learning, well-being, and variation in developmental experiences. High potential for securing extramural research funding to support innovative research on human development across diverse populations and settings. Evidence of or potential for excellence in teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students from a wide range of backgrounds in areas such as human development, developmental science, developmental research methods, and related topics. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT-Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT-Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and many other academic and research units on campus. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT-Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Application Instructions Review of applications will begin 10/19/2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, please provide all requested materials by 10/16/2026. Applicants should upload the following items to the Interfolio system: Cover Letter with contact information Full C.V. Research Statement that includes research accomplishments and future research direction Teaching and Mentoring Statement that describes specific methods used to ensure effective student mentoring and a classroom environment that is welcoming, engaging, and supportive of student learning and success. Up to three representative publications A list of three references with contact information For additional information about the department, see https://education.utexas.edu/departments/educational-psychology/ For questions regarding the application process, please contact Becky Cook ( Becky.Cook@austin.utexas.edu ). Questions about the positions can be directed to the chair of the search committee: Laura Quiñones-Camacho ( laura.quinonescamacho@austin.utexas.edu ) Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Educational Psychology Posted Sep 9, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191411": {
      "title": "Tenured Professor (Nurse Scientist)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Nursing Tenured Professor (Nurse Scientist) Apply now Description The University of Texas at Austin School of Nursing invites applications for senior faculty positions. We seek nationally recognized nurse scientists with established, externally funded research programs and strong records of scholarship, mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Successful candidates will be appointed as tenured Professors, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Exceptional candidates currently at the associate professor level who demonstrate a record consistent with advancement to professor at The University of Texas at Austin are also encouraged to apply. The University of Texas at Austin offers an exceptional environment for interdisciplinary research and collaboration. Faculty have opportunities to engage with scholars, clinicians, and research teams across the University, including Dell Medical School, and its numerous research centers and institutes. The University supports more than 3,500 externally sponsored projects, 150 dedicated research units and centers, and annual research funding exceeding $500 million. The School of Nursing offers BSN, MSN, PhD, and DNP degrees in nursing. Additionally, the School has an alternate entry MSN for persons with baccalaureate degrees outside of nursing, and an accelerated BSN to PhD and BSN to DNP options. The School is committed to advancing nursing science through innovative research, education, clinical practice, and service. The University of Texas at Austin is located in the capitol of Texas. Austin is widely recognized as an innovative and dynamic city offering a high quality of life, abundant cultural and recreational opportunities, and thriving academic, healthcare, and technology sectors. The city has been consistently rated a national creative center that draws talented people from across the world. As an added benefit, the children of professional nurse faculty who are under the age of 25 years are eligible for a full tuition waiver at The University of Texas at Austin. Qualifications Successful Candidates are Expected To: Maintain and advance a nationally recognized, externally funded research program in their areas of expertise. Pursue and sustain competitive extramural research funding from federal agencies, foundations, and other sponsors. Produce high-impact scholarship and contribute to the advancement and translation of nursing science. Develop interdisciplinary and cross-sector collaborations within the School of Nursing, across The University of Texas at Austin, and with the broader healthcare and research community. Mentor junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, doctoral students, and other emerging nurse scientists. Teach and advise graduate and/or undergraduate students consistent with the needs of the School and the candidate’s expertise. Provide leadership and service to the School of Nursing, the University, the community, and the profession. Required Qualifications: Earned research doctorate in nursing. An established record of scholarship appropriate for appointment with tenure at the rank of Professor at The University of Texas at Austin A strong record of peer-reviewed, data-based publications relevant to the candidate's primary area of expertise. A demonstrated record of securing and successfully managing extramural research funding. Evidence of successful mentoring of students, junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, and/or other researchers. Preferred Qualifications: An active program of federally funded research. A sustained record of major extramural research funding appropriate to the candidate's area of expertise. Experience building collaborative research teams and mentoring emerging investigators. Research that complements and expands existing strengths within the School of Nursing and University. Eligibility for RN licensure in the State of Texas Application Instructions Applicants should submit a Letter of Interest (LOI) and Curriculum Vitae (CV) via Interfolio. Materials should be addressed to: Kavita Radhakrishnan, PhD, MSEE, RN, FAAN Dean Laura Lee Blanton Chair in Nursing School of Nursing The University of Texas at Austin Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Nursing Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191398": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, tenure-track",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Assistant Professor, tenure-track Apply now Description The Department of Molecular Biosciences at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level in the field of immunology. We welcome applications from outstanding scientists whose areas of interest span the broader field of immunology, including, but not limited to, foundational mechanisms of immune responses, host-pathogen interactions, neuroimmunology, immune interactions with the microbiome, autoimmunity and inflammation, cancer immunology, hematopoiesis and immune system development, structural immunology, and computational/systems immunology. The Department of Molecular Biosciences is composed of a broad faculty whose research interests span many disciplines including biochemistry, molecular biology, structural biology and biophysics, cell and developmental biology, plant biology, microbiology, immunology and infectious diseases, biotechnology, and systems and synthetic biology. We are excited to welcome collaborative scientists who actively interact with our faculty members and take advantage of our state-of-the-art facilities. Successful candidates are expected to establish and sustain a vibrant research program; serve as dedicated teachers and mentors at both the undergraduate and graduate levels; and assist in the administrative and governance functions of the department, the university, and the profession via service on committees as appropriate.  Applicants at the assistant professor level must have plans to develop an exciting independent research program and a strong commitment to graduate and undergraduate education. “What starts here changes the world.” UT Austin offers numerous resources and an exciting and interactive environment to foster creative interdisciplinary research. In addition to their research activities, our faculty are committed to the development of state-of-the art courses and real-world training experiences for undergraduate and graduate students as part of our core educational mission. Successful candidates will be eligible for membership in the Interdisciplinary Life Sciences Graduate Programs , which includes the Microbiology Graduate Program , and collectively recruit a talented pool of graduate students each year. We also have outstanding core facilities, including those within the Center for Biomedical Research Support , the Texas Advanced Computing Center , and the Sauer Center for Structural Biology . The department has strong ties to other research units at UT Austin, including the School of Engineering, School of Pharmacy, Department of Computer Science, Oden Institute for Computational Engineering & Sciences , and Dell Medical School. This position offers excellent start-up funds, salary, and laboratory space in a dynamic and highly interactive research environment in the vibrant city of Austin.  Applicants will also be encouraged to apply for support from the Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas ( CPRIT ). Qualifications Candidates must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in a relevant discipline and have an outstanding record of scientific ability as demonstrated by a strong publication record. Application Instructions Applicants should apply through Interfolio with the following documents: Cover letter that includes research area, qualifications, and contact information Curriculum vitae A statement of research interests (3-pages maximum) Three confidential letters of recommendation are required and will be requested through Interfolio. Review of applications will begin on October 19, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Molecular Biosciences Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191386": {
      "title": "Tenured Professor (Oncology Nursing)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Nursing Tenured Professor (Oncology Nursing) Apply now Description The University of Texas at Austin School of Nursing invites applications for a senior faculty position in oncology nursing science. We seek an accomplished, nationally recognized nurse scientist with an established program of externally funded research and a strong record of scholarship, mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. The successful candidate will be appointed at the rank of tenured Professor, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Exceptional candidates currently at the associate professor level who demonstrate a record consistent with advancement to professor at The University of Texas at Austin are also encouraged to apply. The University of Texas at Austin offers an exceptional environment for interdisciplinary research and collaboration. Faculty have opportunities to engage with scholars, clinicians, and research teams across the University and its numerous research centers and institutes. The University supports more than 3,500 externally sponsored projects, 150 dedicated research units and centers, and annual research funding exceeding $500 million. The School of Nursing offers BSN, MSN, PhD, and DNP degrees in nursing. Additionally, the School has an alternate entry MSN for persons with baccalaureate degrees outside of nursing, and an accelerated BSN to PhD option and BSN to DNP options. The School is committed to advancing nursing science through innovative research, education, clinical practice, and service. The University of Texas at Austin is located in the capitol of Texas. Austin is widely recognized as an innovative and dynamic city offering a high quality of life, abundant cultural and recreational opportunities, and thriving academic, healthcare, and technology sectors. The city has been consistently rated a national creative center that draws talented people from across the world. As an added benefit, the children of professional nurse faculty who are under the age of 25 years are eligible for a full tuition waiver at The University of Texas at Austin. Qualifications Successful Candidates are Expected To: Maintain and advance a nationally recognized, externally funded program of research focused on oncology, cancer prevention, cancer care, cancer survivorship, symptom science, health outcomes, or other areas relevant to oncology nursing and cancer science. Pursue and sustain competitive extramural research funding from federal agencies, foundations, and other sponsors. Produce high-impact scholarship and contribute to the advancement of oncology nursing science. Develop interdisciplinary collaborations within the School of Nursing and across The University of Texas at Austin and the broader healthcare and research community. Mentor junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, doctoral students, and other emerging nurse scientists. Teach and advise graduate and/or undergraduate students consistent with the needs of the School and the candidate’s expertise. Provide leadership and service to the School of Nursing, the University, the community, and the profession. Required Qualifications: Earned research doctorate in nursing or closely related field. An established record of scholarship appropriate for appointment with tenure at the rank of Professor at The University of Texas at Austin A strong record of peer-reviewed, data-based publications A demonstrated record of securing and successfully managing extramural research funding. Evidence of successful mentoring of students, junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, and/or other researchers. Preferred Qualifications: An active program of federally funded research. A sustained record of NIH or other major extramural research funding. Record of leadership in interdisciplinary cancer or oncology research initiatives. Experience building collaborative research teams and mentoring emerging investigators. Research that complements and expands existing strengths within the School of Nursing and University Eligibility for RN licensure in the State of Texas Application Instructions Applicants should upload a Letter of Interest (LOI) and Curriculum Vitae (CV) via Interfolio. Materials should be addressed to: Kavita Radhakrishnan, PhD, MSEE, RN, FAAN Dean Laura Lee Blanton Chair in Nursing School of Nursing The University of Texas at Austin Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Nursing Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191277": {
      "title": "Tenured or Tenure-track Faculty Position in Astronomical Instrumentation",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Tenured or Tenure-track Faculty Position in Astronomical Instrumentation Apply now Description The Department of Astronomy at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for an open rank (assistant, associate, or full professor) faculty position in Astronomical Instrumentation. We welcome applications from candidates with an emphasis on Instrumentation in any field of astronomy, however we will favor applicants planning to contribute to our resources at McDonald Observatory (including the GMT). The search will begin with a target start as early as August 2027 but will continue until the position is filled. Information on departmental and McDonald Observatory resources and research programs can be found at: https://astronomy.utexas.edu . UT Austin is a major founding partner in the consortium for the next-generation 25-m Giant Magellan Telescope (GMT: http://www.gmto.org). Major new facilities are also being developed at the West Texas McDonald Observatory site. UT Austin faculty members have access to the 10-m Hobby-Eberly Telescope equipped with instruments such as the VIRUS wide-field integral field spectrograph, the Low Resolution Spectrograph (LRS2), and the Habitable-zone Planet Finder (HPF) precision-RV spectrograph. McDonald also hosts the HJST 2.7-m telescope with high and low resolution spectroscopic capability spanning the optical (including integral field units) to near infrared; the 2.1-m and 0.8-m telescopes; and a node for the Las Cumbres Observatory (LCO) network. The UT Astronomy Program provides vibrant collaborative opportunities on large astronomical surveys taking place at McDonald Observatory, and many staff play leading roles in surveys using JWST, Roman, HST, TESS, and other national/international facilities. For instrument/technology development, MDO offers easy access on the Austin campus to machine shops, Class-100 clean room spaces, electronics benches, mechanical assembly areas, and optical/photonic test facilities including fiber IFU labs. The in-house large machine shops are equipped with multiple CNC and manual machines and a wire-EDM machine for complex part fabrication, aided by several precision metrology machines. 3D printing machines are available to aid rapid prototyping. MDO's highly experienced Austin-based instrument scientists/engineers are available for collaboration and support for instrument projects. Campus-wide, UT Austin hosts micro-photonics/electronics fabrication/metrology facilities at the Texas Material Institute (https://tmi.utexas.edu/facilities) and the Micro Electronics Research Center (https://mrc.utexas.edu/facilities). Computational resources include the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC: https://www.tacc.utexas.edu), which designs and operates some of the world’s most powerful computing resources, and is known for its leadership in high performance computing, visualization, big data analytics, and machine learning. UT Austin hosts a broad range of Centers and interdisciplinary initiative, including the Center for Planetary Systems Habitability (CPSH), Cosmic Frontier Center (CFC), Wootton Center for Astrophysical Plasma Physics (WCAPP), Weinberg Institute of Theoretical Physics, and the NSF-Simons AI Institute for Cosmic Origins (CosmicAI). The Department of Astronomy is a highly collaborative and collegial community, with a strong culture of mentorship, scientific exchange, and support for early-career faculty. Our faculty, researchers, and students interact across subfield boundaries, and new colleagues are encouraged to develop ambitious new research directions and collaborations across the University. Beyond campus, Austin offers an exceptional quality of life for individuals and families alike, combining the cultural and culinary opportunities of a major city with abundant green space and easy access to the outdoors. Ranked among the top U.S. cities for raising a family, Austin is known for its extensive parks and trail systems, neighborhood pools and swimming holes, family-friendly restaurants and outdoor gathering spaces, and year-round cultural and recreational activities. UT Austin also offers significant resources for faculty with families, including three university Child Development Center locations. Qualifications Applicants should have a Ph.D. in Astronomy, Physics, Planetary Science, or related fields, or an equivalent engineering degree with demonstrated interest in astronomical research. The primary selection criteria will be excellence and leadership in astronomical research with instrumentation as a primary component, as well as a commitment to excellence in teaching and supervision of undergraduate and graduate students. Potential applicant profiles include candidates who will lead development, construction, and/or transformational science with next-generation instrumentation at McDonald Observatory, GMT, and other national/international ground and space-based observational facilities, and candidates who develop technologies for next-generation astronomical facilities. Applicants are encouraged to highlight throughout their application their relevant experiences and skillsets in supporting academic success of all students (e.g., their specific approaches for teaching, mentoring, research and/or scholarly work). Application Instructions Application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Submissions should include: a cover letter, a description of research accomplishments and current and future research plans (limited to 5 pages total), a statement of teaching interests, experience, pedagogical methods, and goals (limited to 2 pages), a Curriculum Vitae, and a list of publications. Candidates are also required to arrange for 3 letters of recommendation to be submitted to the interfolio site. Complete applications will be reviewed starting Nov 16, 2026 , and we will consider applications after that date on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The start date could be as early August 2027. For further information about the position, please contact the search committee at astro_inst_search2026@utlists.utexas.edu or the Department Chair, Professor Karl Gebhardt (chair@astro.as.utexas.edu). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Astronomy Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Nov 16, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191273": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Astronomy",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Astronomy Apply now Description The Department of Astronomy at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level, with an anticipated start date of August 2027. We welcome applications from candidates in all areas of astronomy and astrophysics. We are particularly interested in candidates working in stellar astrophysics, time-domain and transient astrophysics, the Milky Way, the Local Group, and related areas that can take advantage of UT Austin’s exceptional observational resources, including McDonald Observatory and the Giant Magellan Telescope (GMT). Candidates in other areas that complement or expand the department’s existing research strengths are also strongly encouraged to apply. The search is primarily at the Assistant Professor level, although exceptional candidates at more senior ranks may be considered. Information on departmental and McDonald Observatory resources and research programs can be found at: https://astronomy.utexas.edu . UT Austin offers exceptional access to observational, computational, and collaborative resources. The University is a major founding partner in the next-generation 25-m GMT ( http://www.gmto.org ) . Major new facilities are also being developed at the West Texas McDonald Observatory site. UT Austin faculty have access to the 10-m HET, the 2.7-m HJST, the 2.1-m and 0.8-m telescopes, and a node for the Las Cumbres Observatory (LCO) network. Faculty participate in major astronomical surveys and observing programs using facilities including SDSS, JWST, HST, Roman, TESS, and other national and international observatories. UT Austin also provides exceptional computational resources through the Texas Advanced Computing Center ( TACC: https://www.tacc.utexas.edu ), a world leader in high-performance computing, large-scale data analysis, visualization, and machine learning. UT Austin hosts a broad range of centers and interdisciplinary initiatives that provide opportunities for collaboration across astronomy, physics, planetary science, computational science, and artificial intelligence. These include the Center for Planetary Systems Habitability (CPSH), Cosmic Frontier Center (CFC), Wootton Center for Astrophysical Plasma Properties (WCAPP), Weinberg Institute for Theoretical Physics, and the NSF-Simons AI Institute for Cosmic Origins (CosmicAI). The Department of Astronomy is a highly collaborative and collegial community, with a strong culture of mentorship, scientific exchange, and support for early-career faculty. Our faculty, researchers, and students interact across traditional subfield boundaries, and new colleagues are encouraged to develop ambitious new research directions and collaborations across the University. Beyond campus, Austin offers an exceptional quality of life for individuals and families alike, combining the cultural and culinary opportunities of a major city with abundant green space and easy access to the outdoors. Ranked among the top U.S. cities for raising a family, Austin is known for its extensive parks and trail systems, neighborhood pools and swimming holes, family-friendly restaurants and outdoor gathering spaces, and year-round cultural and recreational activities. UT Austin also offers significant resources for faculty with families, including three university Child Development Center locations. Seventy-six percent of Austin residents live within a 10-minute walk of a park, in which the city supports an extensive network of public pools and splash pads. Austin offers an unusually rich quality of life for faculty and their families. Qualifications Applicants should have a Ph.D. in Astronomy, Physics, Planetary Science, or related fields. The primary selection criteria will be excellence and leadership in astrophysics research, teaching, and mentoring of undergraduate and graduate students. We particularly seek candidates who will lead transformational science with next-generation observational and computational facilities and creatively utilize the resources of the Astronomy Department and McDonald Observatory. Applicants are encouraged to highlight throughout their application their relevant experiences and skillsets in supporting academic success of all students (e.g., their specific approaches for teaching, mentoring, research and /or scholarly work). Application Instructions Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio and should include: Cover letter. Statement of research accomplishments and current and future research plans (5 page limit). Candidates should describe how they envision developing their research program at UT Austin, including potential use of relevant university and departmental resources, facilities, observational capabilities, computational resources, and opportunities for collaboration. Statement of teaching experience, pedagogical approaches, and future teaching plans (2 page limit). Candidates should describe pedagogical methods they have used or would like to explore, their experience teaching at different levels, and the courses they would be prepared and/or excited to teach or develop at UT Austin. Curriculum Vitae. List of publications. Three letters of recommendation requested through the application website. Complete applications received by November 10, 2026 will be assured of full consideration. For further information about the position, please contact the search committee at astro_search2025@utlists.utexas.edu or the Department Chair, Professor Karl Gebhardt ( chair@astro.as.utexas.edu ). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Astronomy Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Nov 10, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191272": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Professional Track Faculty Aerospace Engineering & Engineering Mechanics 2026-27 Apply now Description The Department of Aerospace Engineering and Engineering Mechanics at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for professional track full or part-time positions to teach undergraduate and graduate courses.  Positions are contingent on instructional needs.  Appointments are typically made on a semester basis, although longer-term appointments may be available.  Professional-track appointments are non-tenure track and are renewable annually. Qualifications Applicants must possess a graduate degree or equivalent, excellent communication and interpersonal skills along with the ability to teach and work in a collaborative environment.  Preference will be given to applicants with prior university teaching experience, years of experience, and/or graduate degrees. Viable candidates should be capable of teaching a wide range of aerospace engineering courses. An understanding and appreciation for aerospace engineering fundamentals, as well as excellent management, communication, and organizational skills, and a willingness to learn quickly, is required. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Interested applicants should submit a detailed resume including academic, training, and technical experience, a statement regarding their specific teaching interests and qualifications, and a list of three references. Further information about the Aerospace Engineering & Engineering Mechanics Department may be found at ase.utexas.edu . Only complete applications will be considered. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Aerospace Engineering & Engineering Mechanics Posted Sep 4, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191196": {
      "title": "Tenured Faculty Position in Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Tenured Faculty Position in Biomedical Engineering - Fall 2027 Apply now Description The Department of Biomedical Engineering is dedicated to developing solutions for human health by educating the next generation of biomedical engineers, cultivating leaders, and nurturing the integration of engineering, science, and medicine in a discovery-centered environment. To help advance this mission, we are seeking a distinguished applicant to fill one Tenured faculty position at the Associate or Full Professor level. We are focused on recruiting faculty into two targeted areas: neuroengineering and bioelectronics. Qualifications Required qualifications include a doctorate in engineering or a related field with an outstanding record of research accomplishments, publications, and appropriate externally sponsored funding. Candidates are also expected to have a successful record of mentoring the new generation of leaders in biomedical engineering, strong interest in undergraduate and graduate teaching in biomedical engineering, and a commitment to university and professional service.  Additionally, we seek applicants who have a desire to build collaborations with Dell Medical School and other regional medical centers including MD Anderson Cancer Center, UT Southwestern Medical Center at Dallas, the Texas Heart Institute, and UT Health Science Centers at Houston and San Antonio. Candidates whose research aligns with Texas funding mechanisms such as CPRIT (Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas) and the recently approved DPRIT (Dementia Prevention Research Institute of Texas) are encouraged to apply. Application Instructions Interested persons should include a cover letter expressing interest; a detailed curriculum vitae including academic and professional experience and peer reviewed publications (please include PDF copies of up to three impactful peer-reviewed publications); a statement of research; a statement of teaching goals as related to our departmental focus; and the names and e-mail addresses of at least five references. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Successful candidates will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the U.S. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Please upload your documents via our Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/193056 Please direct inquiries to Brittain Sobey at b.sobey@utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Biomedical Engineering Posted Sep 4, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191235": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions McCombs School of Business 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty - IROM Apply now Description The IROM Department periodically hires professional track faculty in all four areas of the department: Information Management, Decision Sciences, Statistics, and Operations Management. This includes hiring in the Lecturer and Clinical series of titles. Qualifications Candidates should have at least a bachelor's degree in the specialization to be taught, have potential for excellence in teaching at the undergraduate level, and demonstrate excellent communication skills. Advanced degrees are preferred for senior and research titles in each series. Experience in industry may be substituted when appropriate. Positions are not available every semester, and are contingent on funding . Application Instructions Upload Curriculum Vitae and reference information for three letters of recommendation that emphasize the potential for success as a teacher to Interfolio. If you are applying for a position that includes research, letters of recommendation should discuss your previous research work and potential for future success as a researcher and teacher. Applications not uploaded to Interfolio will not be considered. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit McCombs School of Business Department Department of Information, Risk, & Operations Management (IROM) Posted Sep 3, 2026 Apply by Aug 31, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191191": {
      "title": "FY 26-27 Non-Tenure Track Faculty Position in Theatre and Dance",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Fine Arts FY 26-27 Non-Tenure Track Faculty Position in Theatre and Dance Apply now Description The Department of Theatre and Dance offers a unique opportunity for professional development in teaching, creative practice and research. Positions are available periodically for non-tenure track faculty who are interested in teaching and who have the potential to contribute to the development of the academic programs and creative activity in the areas of Acting, Directing, Drama and Theatre for Youth and Communities, Scene Design, Costume Design, Lighting Design, Costume Technology, Theatre Technology, Performance as Public Practice, Dance, and Playwriting. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent on funding. The University of Texas at Austin and the Department of Theatre and Dance is committed to fostering a welcoming community. We welcome applicants who can help achieve our objectives. Qualifications Qualifications must be commensurate with rank and include an outstanding academic record, significant achievement in original research/creative activity/exhibition record and a commitment of quality teaching. The successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate courses, collaborate with other faculty, and be involved in professional/creative research. Application Instructions Interested applicants are invited to upload a letter of interest, curriculum vita, and the names and contact information of three references. All materials should be submitted by clicking the \"Apply Now\" button, below. Please do not send additional supporting materials until requested. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Fine Arts Department Department of Theatre & Dance Posted Sep 3, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/190886": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice & Program Director in Applied Data Analytics",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Assistant Professor of Practice & Program Director in Applied Data Analytics Apply now Description The Department of Educational Psychology (EDP) in the College of Education at The University of Texas at Austin seeks applications for a full-time professional-track Program Director for a new Option III Master’s program in Educational Psychology with a specialization in Applied Data Analytics. This innovative professional master’s program is designed to serve working professionals and prepare graduates to use data to inform decision-making across education, health, policy, and related applied contexts through a flexible, online format. This is a non-tenure track position. The Program Director will provide academic and administrative leadership for the program and play a central role in its launch, growth, and long-term success. This position offers a distinctive opportunity to shape and lead a high-impact professional graduate program at a flagship R1 institution. UT's Department of Educational Psychology has robust graduate programs and is currently ranked #6 among the Best Educational Psychology Programs rated by U.S. News & World Report . The department advances knowledge about the human experience at the intersection of education and psychology through research, teaching, and practice. It provides an interdisciplinary and contemporary approach to learning and development and seeks to enhance the quality of the human experience through rigorous educational and psychological research, and effective translation of findings to policy and practice. Responsibilities Provide strategic and operational leadership for the Option III Master’s program. Oversee curriculum implementation and continuous refinement in collaboration with departmental faculty and college leadership. Recruit, advise, and support graduate students, including working professionals and non-traditional learners. Coordinate course scheduling, instructional staffing, and instructional quality for online courses. Lead program assessment, reporting, and continuous improvement efforts. Support marketing, outreach, and enrollment management in collaboration with the College and Graduate School. Teach graduate-level courses in the Master’s program, including one or more of the following: Fundamentals of Probability and Statistics; Data Analytics: Fundamentals and Ethics; Data Wrangling and Preprocessing; Data Visualization; Regression Methods; Machine Learning for Applied Research; Applications in Data Mining; Social Network Analysis in Applied Research; Longitudinal Data Analytics; and Fundamentals of Text Analytics. Qualifications REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in Data Science, Data Analytics, Quantitative Methods, Quantitative Psychology, Statistics, Applied Statistics, or a closely related field earned by January 1, 2027 Demonstrated expertise in applied quantitative methods, data analytics, or related domains. Experience with or strong potential for graduate-level instruction and/or professional training in quantitative or data-focused areas. Experience with or strong potential for leadership, program coordination, or administrative experience in academic or professional settings. Strong organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills. Appointment Details This is a full-time professional-track appointment. The position includes administrative responsibilities with associated support (e.g., course releases or equivalent workload adjustments). In addition to leadership of the Option III program, the Program Director will carry an instructional load that includes formula-funded courses in the Department of Educational Psychology. The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with the College of Natural Sciences and with many other academic and research units on campus including Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and the Population Research Center. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted via Interfolio and should include the following documents: Letter of application with stated qualifications Curriculum vitae Contact information including title for three references Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Pre-employment screening includes verification of academic credentials and background check. For additional information about the department, see https://education.utexas.edu/departments/educational-psychology/ For questions regarding the application process, please contact Becky Cook ( Becky.Cook@austin.utexas.edu ). Questions about the positions can be directed to the chair of the search committee: Dr. Tiffany Whittaker ( t.whittaker@austin.utexas.edu ). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Educational Psychology Posted Sep 3, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191121": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts UTeach Liberal Arts - 2026-27 Professional-Track Faculty Pool Apply now Description UTeach-Liberal Arts at The University of Texas at Austin seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/nontenured positions to teach and supervise undergraduate, post-baccalaureate and graduate students seeking English, History, Social Studies and Languages Other Than English (LOTE) certifications. Qualifications Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Must hold, or have held, teacher certification in a liberal arts field. Fifteen years of experience teaching in middle school or high school, as administrator, or evaluator in EC-12 settings with preference for public school experience. Experience developing academic curriculum, leading professional development, and demonstrated expertise with educational technology. Excellent interpersonal communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s Degree or PhD. Demonstrated potential to supervise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students at a large research university with a diverse student body. Training and experience in social emotional learning and trauma-informed instruction as related to education. Knowledge of the Texas Teacher Standards. Active participation in professional organizations and activities. More than above stated qualifications. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae, Letter of Interest, and List of 3 References in order to apply. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department UTeach - Liberal Arts Posted Sep 2, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191107": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Rhetoric and Writing - 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty Pool Apply now Description The Department of Rhetoric and Writing at The University of Texas at Austin seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/nontenured positions to teach undergraduate courses. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Appointments usually are made on a semester-by-semester basis, though sometimes the department has a need for an annual appointment. Positions are renewable annually, contingent upon budgetary funding and performance. Qualifications A PhD in a related field and a record of teaching excellence at the university level are preferred. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit a current curriculum vitae, the names and contact information of three professional references, and any course materials (syllabi, course evaluations, etc.). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Rhetoric & Writing Posted Sep 2, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191095": {
      "title": "Tenured Professor & Division Head",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Pharmacy Tenured Professor & Division Head - Chemical Biology & Medicinal Chemistry 26-27 Apply now Description The Division of Chemical Biology and Medicinal Chemistry (CBMC) at The University of Texas at Austin College of Pharmacy works at the nexus of Chemistry and Biology to generate chemical and biochemical tools to study mechanisms of cell signaling, biosynthetic pathways, enzyme evolution DNA repair, and other therapeutic targets for drug design. To advance this mission, CBMC invites applications for a full-time tenured faculty position at the Professor level who will also serve as Division Head effective August 2027. CBMC is a highly interdisciplinary and interactive group of seven faculty members engaged in cutting edge research. The successful applicant will engage in productive interdisciplinary research, provide high-quality teaching, and actively participate in university service. Qualifications Required qualifications include a Ph.D. in a relevant field and a strong record of vigorous research accomplishments including a career stage appropriate funded research program, and publications, with a focus on chemical and molecular mechanisms of high biomedical significance. Qualified candidates in research areas that would be eligible for CPRIT (Cancer Prevention Research Institute of Texas) and/or DPRIT (Dementia Prevention Research Institute of Texas) Established Investigator awards, are especially encouraged to apply. To ensure success and longevity, competitive start-up packages are provided, with potential endowment recognition for the successful candidate. Candidates are also expected to teach core professional and graduate courses in Pharmacy, including the Pharm.D. program, develop and nurture a sponsored research program, supervise graduate students, collaborate with other faculty, and have a commitment to university and professional service. Additionally, we seek applicants who have a desire to build collaborations with Dell Medical School, the UT Austin Cancer Research Center, the LaMontagne Center for Infectious Disease, and others. Furthermore, the college has strong clinical partnerships with several medical institutions across Texas, including the UT Health/UT San Antonio. Application Instructions Interested persons should include a letter of interest; a detailed curriculum vitae; a statement of research (including current and planned funding); a statement of teaching philosophy and interests; and the names and e-mail addresses of at least three arm's length references for rank of Tenured Full Professor . Applications received by December 1, 2026 will be given priority review, but the position will remain open until filled. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Pharmacy Department Division of Chemical Biology and Medicinal Chemistry Posted Sep 2, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
