    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/190544": {
      "title": "Non-Tenure Track Faculty Position in Art & Art History 26-27",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Fine Arts Non-Tenure Track Faculty Position in Art & Art History 26-27 Apply now Description The Department of Art and Art History offers a unique opportunity for professional development in teaching, creative practice, and research.  Positions are available periodically for non-tenure track faculty who are interested in teaching and who have the potential to contribute to the development of the academic programs and creative activity in the areas of Studio Art, Art Education, and Art History.  Positions are not available every semester and are contingent on funding. Qualifications Qualifications must be commensurate with rank and include an outstanding academic record, significant achievement in original research/creative activity/exhibition record, and a commitment to quality teaching.  The successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate courses, collaborate with other faculty, and be active in professional/creative research. The University of Texas at Austin and the Department of Art and Art History is committed to fostering a welcoming community. We welcome applicants who can help achieve this objective. Application Instructions Interested applicants are invited to upload a letter of interest, curriculum vita, and the names and contact information of three references.  All materials should be submitted by clicking the \"Apply Now for Free\" button, below.  Applications will be accepted through August 15, 2027.  Please do not send additional supporting materials until requested. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Fine Arts Department Department of Art & Art History Posted Aug 25, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/189936": {
      "title": "International Economic Policy (Assistant/Advanced Assistant Professor)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions LBJ School of Public Affairs International Economic Policy (Assistant/Advanced Assistant Professor) Apply now Description The Lyndon B. Johnson School of Public Affairs at the University of Texas at Austin (“LBJ School”) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant or Advanced Assistant Professor of International Economic Policy to begin in Fall of academic year 2027-28 (with an August 2027 start date). One of the nation’s top programs in public affairs, the LBJ School is seeking to strengthen its faculty in international economics and trade. This faculty would support teaching in the Ph.D. in Public Policy, Master of National Security, Master of Global Policy Studies, and/or Bachelor of Arts and Master in Public Affairs. The appointee would teach courses on topics such as international economics and trade, international finance and monetary policy, development economics and industrial policy. Topics related to direct foreign investment, supply chain resilience, and commercial diplomacy would also be valued. Faculty research may focus on trade, geoeconomics, development economics, economic geography, international political economy, international macroeconomics and finance, labor economics of migration and remittances, global value chains and supply chain economics, foreign direct investment and multinationals, trade and climate, industrial policy. Candidates with prior applied policy research or related work experience will be viewed in a positive light in the search process.  The appointee will conduct and publish scholarly research and seek grant opportunities in support of their research and/or teaching activities. The appointee will participate in faculty governance at the LBJ School. Qualifications A Ph.D. in Economics, Finance, Political Economy, Political Science, or a related field is required. ABD candidates will be considered if the doctoral degree will be completed by the time of employment. Experience in college teaching and evidence of scholarship or scholarly potential is preferred. Application Instructions Interested candidates can submit their cover letter, CV, statement of teaching, three years of teaching evaluations (or fewer if unavailable), three letters of reference, and one article-length writing sample via Interfolio. Questions about the position can be directed to Andrew Waxman at awaxman@utexas.edu . Questions about the application can be directed to Shannon LaFevers at slafevers@austin.utexas.edu. The search committee will begin considering applications November 8, 2026 . The position will remain open until filled. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit LBJ School of Public Affairs Posted Aug 25, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/190413": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Lithospheric Deformation",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Jackson School of Geosciences Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Lithospheric Deformation Apply now Description The Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences (EPS) at The University of Texas at Austin (UT) seeks to hire an Assistant Professor (tenure-track) or Associate Professor (tenured) in the field of Lithospheric Deformation. We seek a colleague with an innovative research and education vision, who will add to existing strengths in our department in structural geology, tectonics, geomechanics, and lithospheric dynamics. Example areas of specialization include, but are not limited to: structural geology and deformation processes; tectonic, glacial, planetary, injection-induced, volcanic collapse, or landslide-related deformation; fault zone geology and links to earthquake processes; geomechanics; seismotectonics; and structural and deformation processes applied to industry sectors such as energy, critical minerals, and geothermal exploration. The successful applicant will contribute to a multidisciplinary research community within the Jackson School of Geosciences by building and leading an innovative, vibrant, and externally funded research program that complements existing research strengths in solid Earth and tectonic processes. In addition, the candidate will contribute to our interdisciplinary teaching community through teaching and mentoring our large graduate and undergraduate student bodies. Candidates will be expected to demonstrate interest or experience in teaching structural geology and tectonics as part of field educational programs. The EPS department (https://eps.jsg.utexas.edu/) is part of the Jackson School of Geosciences (JSG) (https://www.jsg.utexas.edu); the department is home to over 50 faculty and a community of research scientists with a broad range of specialties, as well as access to outstanding research facilities and support. The School has one of the largest combined graduate and undergraduate enrollments of any Earth science program in North America. EPS is closely allied with the University of Texas Institute for Geophysics (UTIG) and the Bureau of Economic Geology (BEG), two large research units within the JSG. The Jackson School also partners with other entities across the University, including the Oden Institute for Computational Engineering and Sciences, the Cockrell School of Engineering, the College of Natural Sciences, the Texas Advanced Computing Center, the Center for Planetary Systems Habitability, and the Center for Space Research. The School also maintains extensive external partnerships nationally and internationally. The university is located in the thriving Austin metropolitan area with a dynamic, multicultural community of over 2 million people. Qualifications A Ph.D. is required at the time of appointment. The successful applicant is expected to establish a vibrant, externally funded research program. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit a cover letter, CV including list of publications, two to three- page research statement outlining how the applicant’s expertise addresses the above criteria and their future research plans, a two-page teaching and mentoring statement, and a list of three individuals who would be able to provide letters of reference. Submit copies of these materials online via Interfolio’s ‘Apply Now’ option. The University of Texas at Austin is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Review of applications will begin October 19th, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Questions concerning the search can be addressed to Jaime Barnes (jdbarnes@jsg.utexas.edu), and about the application and submissions process to EPS at EPSoffice@jsg.utexas.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Jackson School of Geosciences Department Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences Posted Aug 24, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/189940": {
      "title": "Health Policy (Assistant Professor)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions LBJ School of Public Affairs Health Policy (Assistant Professor) Apply now Description The Lyndon B. Johnson School of Public Affairs at the University of Texas at Austin (“LBJ School”) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Health Policy to begin in Fall of academic year 2027-28 (with an August 2027 start date). One of the nation’s top programs in public affairs, the LBJ School is seeking to strengthen its faculty in health policy design, implementation, and evaluation. The LBJ School comprises more than 50 faculty members and approximately 350 graduate students, and a new undergraduate degree that will eventually serve another 500 students. It offers multi-disciplinary curricula leading to the degrees of Bachelor of Arts of Public Affairs, Master of Public Affairs, Master of Global Policy Studies, Master of National Security, and Ph.D. in Public Policy. The ideal candidate will possess proven experience and expertise in one or more of the following areas: healthcare systems, healthcare policy reforms, health services research, and the effects of policy on health (e.g. policies affecting the socioeconomic or environmental determinants of health). They will also teach courses related to public policy. The appointee’s research will have direct relevance to public policy. The appointee will conduct and publish scholarly research and seek grant opportunities in support of their research and/or teaching activities. The appointee will participate in faculty governance in the LBJ School. Qualifications A Ph.D. in Economics, Public Policy, Political Science, Statistics/Data Science, Sociology, Public Health or a related field with an emphasis in health policy is required. ABD candidates will be considered if the doctoral degree will be complete by the time of employment. Candidates who are already Assistant Professors will be considered as well.  Experience in college teaching and evidence of scholarship or scholarly potential is preferred. Application Instructions Interested candidates can submit their cover letter, CV, statement of teaching, three years of teaching evaluations (or fewer if unavailable), three letters of reference, and one article-length writing sample via Interfolio. Questions about the position can be directed to Abigail Aiken at araa2@utexas.edu. Questions about the application process can be directed to Shannon LaFevers at slafevers@austin.utexas.edu The search committee will begin considering applications November 8th, 2026 . The position will remain open until filled, but applications submitted before November 8th will receive priority. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit LBJ School of Public Affairs Posted Aug 18, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/189937": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Film and Media Industry Studies",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Moody College of Communication Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Film and Media Industry Studies Apply now Description The Department of Radio-Television-Film at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a full-time tenure-track assistant professor to begin in Fall 2027. The Department is part of the top-ranked Moody College of Communication , which includes programs in Advertising and Public Relations, Communication Studies, Journalism and Media, and Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences. We welcome applications from scholars conducting historical and/or contemporary research and specializing in film, television, video games, social media, music, and/or other media industries. We conceive industry studies broadly to include production studies, policy studies, political economy, labor studies, and infrastructure studies.  Applicants must demonstrate potential for excellence in scholarship, as well as in teaching a range of required and elective courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels, graduate mentoring, and university service. Candidates for this faculty position should be prepared to teach courses currently offered by the department as well as develop new courses aligned with their research interests. The Department of Radio-Television-Film highlights a range of areas including film and television history and production, critical/cultural analysis, digital studies, global media, industries, screenwriting, and more. Qualifications Applicants must have completed a Ph.D. at the time of the appointment. Applicants should have a promising or established program of research and publication record, a commitment to teaching excellence, and an interest in advising and mentoring graduate students and undertaking collaborative research. The ability to actively pursue and secure external funding is also valued. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, statements on teaching and research (each statement no more than 500 words), and the names and contact information for three references. Letters of recommendation will be requested of finalists. Screening of applicants will begin October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must be made via Interfolio. If you do not have a Dossier account with Interfolio, you will be prompted to create one prior to applying for the position. If you have questions about using Interfolio, please email help@interfolio.com or call (877) 997-8807. Questions can be directed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Alisa Perren at aperren@austin.utexas.edu Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Moody College of Communication Department Department of Radio-Television-Film Posted Aug 18, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/189919": {
      "title": "Professor of Finance (Assistant, Associate, and Full) 2026-27",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions McCombs School of Business Professor of Finance (Assistant, Associate, and Full) 2026-27 Apply now Description Applications are invited from qualified individuals who have excellent research and teaching capabilities. Ph.D. (or very near completion) is required. We will also consider appointments at the Associate and Full Professor rank. A strong publication record in the top-tier finance and economics journals is required for appointments at senior ranks. Successful applicants will be expected to establish an internationally recognized research program at the University of Texas, mentor graduate students, and teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The predominant mode of instruction and research is in-person, such that faculty appointed for employment shall reside in the proximate area of the work site for the duration of their appointment and be able to maintain a daily in-person schedule at the assigned work site. Qualifications Ph.D. (or very near completion) before start time is required.  A strong publication record in the top-tier finance and economics journals is required for appointments at senior ranks. Application Instructions Submit cover letter, CV, papers and publications, and a minimum of three letters of recommendation via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/191750 .  Screening of applications is open immediately upon this posting and to receive full consideration, complete applications must be received by Sunday, November 8th, 2026. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit McCombs School of Business Department Department of Finance Posted Aug 18, 2026 Apply by Nov 8, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
